= Glossary of Foreign Terms

_Back-matter reference. This glossary defines every foreign-language technical term used in the substance entries. Organized by tradition. Within each section, terms are grouped thematically — fundamentals, pharmacological framework, action vocabulary, drug-form vocabulary, anatomical-physiological terms, formula classes, named-disease vocabulary, source-text titles — and alphabetized within each group. Closing section: cross-tradition concordance mapping convergent concepts across systems._

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫)

=== Fundamental concepts

*陰陽 (yīn yáng)* — the foundational dialectical-polar framework. _Yin_: dark, cold, internal, descending, substantial, feminine. _Yang_: bright, hot, external, ascending, functional, masculine. Health is dynamic balance; disease is dyscrasia. Foundational to every later concept.

*氣 (qì)* — vital energy / breath / functional force. Many sub-classifications: 元氣 (yuán qì, source qi), 宗氣 (zōng qì, gathering qi), 營氣 (yíng qì, nutritive qi), 衛氣 (wèi qì, defensive qi), 臟腑之氣 (organ-specific qi). The basic functional substance of the body.

*血 (xuè)* — blood; carries both the modern hematological substance and the broader Chinese-medicine concept of the nutritive yin-substance circulating with qi.

*津液 (jīn yè)* — body fluids. _Jīn_: thin clear fluids (sweat, tears, saliva). _Yè_: thick turbid fluids (joint synovium, marrow, gastric secretions). Together the body's water-and-fluid economy.

*精 (jīng)* — essence; the foundational substance of growth, reproduction, and constitutional vitality. Stored in the Kidney; depleted by age, exertion, sexual activity; tonified by foods, herbs, and lifestyle.

*神 (shén)* — spirit / consciousness / mental-emotional being; housed in the Heart. Disturbance of shén appears as anxiety, insomnia, mania.

*五行 (wǔ xíng, "five phases / five elements")* — the five-phase correspondence framework: 木 (mù, Wood) → 火 (huǒ, Fire) → 土 (tǔ, Earth) → 金 (jīn, Metal) → 水 (shuǐ, Water) → back to Wood. Each phase corresponds to organs, seasons, colors, flavors, emotions, climates, sounds. Foundational to organ-system pathology. Source: _Su Wen_ ch. 5.

*藥食同源 (yào shí tóng yuán, "medicine and food share the same source")* — the foundational Chinese dietetic principle that many substances function simultaneously as food and as drug, with no sharp category boundary between the two; the premise underlying this entire book's materia-medica approach to foods. Applies with particular cleanness to grain-and-seed drugs eaten both as staple food and prescribed as medicine, e.g. barley, adzuki, and especially 薏苡仁 (Job's tears) — see _coix.typ_.

=== Diagnostic categories

*六淫 (liù yín, "six external excesses")* — the six external pathogenic factors: 風 (fēng, wind), 寒 (hán, cold), 暑 (shǔ, summer-heat), 濕 (shī, damp), 燥 (zào, dryness), 火 (huǒ, fire). Each has organ-system correspondence (wind→Liver; cold→Kidney; damp→Spleen; dryness→Lung; fire→Heart). The six-excesses framework is the foundational climatic-pathogenic system; see _climates.typ_.

*七情 (qī qíng, "seven emotions")* — the seven internal pathogenic emotions: 喜 (joy), 怒 (anger), 思 (worry), 憂 (sorrow), 悲 (grief), 恐 (fear), 驚 (fright). Each disturbs a specific organ.

*八綱 (bā gāng, "eight principles")* — the diagnostic-categorization framework: 陰 yin / 陽 yang, 表 biǎo (exterior) / 裡 lǐ (interior), 寒 hán (cold) / 熱 rè (hot), 虛 xū (vacuity) / 實 shí (excess). Used to organize disease patterns.

*臟腑 (zàng fǔ, "viscera-and-bowels")* — the organ-system framework. _Five zàng_ (yin / solid organs storing essence): 心 (Heart), 肝 (Liver), 脾 (Spleen), 肺 (Lung), 腎 (Kidney). _Six fǔ_ (yang / hollow organs processing substance): 膽 (Gallbladder), 胃 (Stomach), 小腸 (Small Intestine), 大腸 (Large Intestine), 膀胱 (Bladder), 三焦 (Triple Burner — the "extra" fu, governing fluid transport). Each zàng paired with a fǔ.

*運化 (yùn huà, "transportation and transformation")* — the *Spleen's governing function*: transforming food and fluid into qi and blood and distributing them through the body. Its failure is the mechanism of 脾氣虛 (Spleen-qi vacuity), and it maps loosely — as an analogy, not an identity — onto modern notions of digestive, absorptive and gut-barrier function. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*脾虛生濕 (pí xū shēng shī, "Spleen vacuity generates damp")* — the maxim that a failing Spleen leaves food and fluid untransformed, the residue becoming *damp*, which then further burdens the organ that failed to transform it: a *vicious circle*, not a side effect. The reason supplementing formulas for the Spleen must also dry and drain — see 補中有瀉 and _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*三焦 (sān jiāo, "Triple Burner")* — a uniquely Chinese-medicine organ-system covering the upper (above diaphragm, Heart-Lung), middle (between diaphragm and umbilicus, Spleen-Stomach), and lower (below umbilicus, Kidney-Bladder-Intestines) regions; governs water-and-fluid transport. No anatomical Western equivalent.

*歸經 (guī jīng, "channel-entry / channel-affinity")* — the specification, for each drug, of which of the twelve channels (jīng-luò) it preferentially affects. The four parameters of Chinese pharmacology are 性 + 味 + 升降浮沉 + 歸經.

*十八反 (shíbā fǎn, "the eighteen antagonisms")* — the canonical list of *eighteen drug pairs held to counteract one another*, transmitted as a mnemonic verse and standard in formula pedagogy. The line concerning ginseng runs *諸參辛芍叛藜蘆* — "all the _shen_ [ginseng and its namesakes], _xin_ [asarum], and _shao_ [peony] rebel against 藜蘆 (_Veratrum_)." Together with the 十九畏 it constitutes classical 配伍禁忌 (combining prohibitions). See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*十九畏 (shíjiǔ wèi, "the nineteen mutual fears")* — the companion canonical list of *nineteen pairs in which one drug "fears" (畏) another*, i.e. has its action blunted or distorted by it. Examples: *人參畏五靈脂* (ginseng fears _Trogopterus_ dung) and *丁香畏郁金* (clove fears turmeric). See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _cloves.typ_.

*暑傷氣 (shǔ shāng qì, "summer-heat damages qi")* — the classical doctrine that profuse sweating in humid heat *depletes qi and fluids*, leaving the exterior hot while the interior becomes depleted and cold — hence the counterintuitive propriety of *warming tonification in summer*. The rationale for 生脈散, the 清暑益氣 formula class, and the Korean *삼계탕* custom. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _climates.typ_.

*以熱治熱 (yǐ rè zhì rè, "treat heat with heat")* — Korean *이열치열*: the principle, following from 暑傷氣, that hot tonifying food and drink are appropriate in the hottest season, because the depletion to be corrected is interior and cold. Not folk paradox but a derived classical rule; the canonical instance is ginseng-chicken soup eaten on the three hottest days of the Korean year. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _climates.typ_.

*發物 (fā wù, "provoking foods")* — the living folk-clinical category of foods held to *provoke or re-surface latent disease* — especially skin disease, suppuration, allergy, and inflammatory conditions — and therefore avoided during such illness and after surgery. Fish, shellfish, lamb, and certain pungent vegetables are the usual members. Still widely observed in Chinese practice; a *folk-clinical rather than strictly textual* category, and the close analogue of the Tibb *muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ* (humour-stirring) caution and of the Ayurvedic contraindication of fish in _kuṣṭha_. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*寒熱虛實 (hán-rè xū-shí)* — the four-axis pattern descriptor: cold-vs-hot and vacuity-vs-excess. Combined with the six channels and the five-phase framework to generate specific patterns.

=== Pharmacological framework — the four parameters of a Chinese drug

*性 / 氣 (xìng / qì, "property / qi")* — the thermal classification of a drug, on five degrees: 寒 (hán, cold) → 涼 (liáng, cool) → 平 (píng, neutral) → 溫 (wēn, warm) → 熱 (rè, hot). The principal axis for cooling-vs-warming drug-pattern matching.

*味 (wèi, "flavor / taste")* — the five-flavor classification: 甘 (gān, sweet), 酸 (suān, sour), 苦 (kǔ, bitter), 辛 (xīn, pungent / acrid), 鹹 (xián, salty). Sometimes 淡 (dàn, bland) and 澀 (sè, astringent) are added. Each flavor has organ-system affinity and specific action.

*歸經 (guī jīng)* — channel affinity; see above.

*淡味滲泄 (dàn wèi shèn xiè, "the bland flavour percolates and discharges")* — the classical principle that *blandness is a positive pharmacological category*, not merely an absence of taste: a bland drug drains dampness through urination. 淡 is one of the two flavours (with 澀, astringent) added to the canonical five. The principle governs the two great bland-sweet damp-drainers, 茯苓 and 薏苡仁 — and it has *no counterpart among the six Ayurvedic rasas*, a genuine structural asymmetry between the two pharmacologies. See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*三品 (sān pǐn, "the three grades")* — the _Shennong Bencao Jing_'s tripartite classification of drugs, and a clinical instruction rather than a ranking of quality. *上品 (shàng pǐn,* superior) — nourishing, non-toxic, safe for prolonged use, the longevity-and-constitution class (ginseng, poria, chrysanthemum, Job's tears, sesame). *中品 (zhōng pǐn,* middle) — tonifying or treating by turns, requiring judgement. *下品 (xià pǐn,* inferior) — *treats disease, possesses toxicity, not to be taken over long periods* (半夏, aconite). A 下品 classification marks a drug as potent and time-limited, not as inferior medicine. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*有毒 (yǒu dú, "toxic")* — a *formal property* of a Chinese drug, stated in its entry alongside 性 (property) and 味 (flavour), and graded in the literature as 小毒 / 有毒 / 大毒 (slight, toxic, greatly toxic). It is a prescribing parameter, not a warning appended by modern editors: a 有毒 drug is expected to be processed (炮製), dose-limited, time-limited, and usually corrected by a companion drug. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*升降浮沉 (shēng-jiàng fú-chén, "ascending-descending floating-sinking")* — the directional / depth tendency of a drug's action. _Shēng_ (ascending) and _fú_ (floating) drugs act outward and upward; _jiàng_ (descending) and _chén_ (sinking) drugs act inward and downward. The fourth Chinese pharmacological parameter.

=== Action vocabulary (representative — not exhaustive)

*補 (bǔ)* — tonify; the action of building / supplementing what is deficient. 補氣 (qi-tonifying), 補血 (blood-tonifying), 補陰 (yin-tonifying), 補陽 (yang-tonifying).

*大補元氣 (dà bǔ yuán qì, "greatly tonifies the source qi")* — the strongest tonifying action in the pharmacy, reserved for *氣虛欲脫* (qi vacuity verging on collapse): cold sweat, cold limbs, a pulse 微欲絕 ("faint and about to expire"). Effectively the signature of a single drug, 人參 — whence *獨參湯* (Dú Shēn Tāng, "Ginseng Alone Decoction"), ginseng in large dose as a *single-herb* emergency formula. Distinguish from ordinary 補氣. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*回陽救逆 (huí yáng jiù nì, "restores yang and rescues from collapse")* — the shock-therapy action category: for yang collapse with cold limbs, faint pulse, and clouded consciousness. The canonical drugs are 附子 (aconite) and 乾薑, with 人參 added in 四逆加人參湯 and 參附湯. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _ginger.typ_.

*安神益智 (ān shén yì zhì, "calms the spirit and benefits the wits")* — the paired spirit-settling and cognition-supporting action; the register of 歸脾湯 and 天王補心丹, and the 中醫 counterpart of the Ayurvedic _medhya_ + _manaḥ-praśamana_ pairing. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _fuling.typ_.

*生津止渴 (shēng jīn zhǐ kě, "generates fluids and allays thirst")* — the fluid-restoring action, for 消渴 (consumptive thirst) and for heat- or summer-damage to fluids; the register of 生脈散 and 白虎加人參湯. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _schisandra.typ_, _pear.typ_.

*瀉 (xiè)* — drain / purge; the action of expelling what is excess. Often paired dialectically with 補.

*清 (qīng)* — clear; the action of removing heat or toxin without forcing it out. 清熱 (clears heat), 清熱解毒 (clears heat and resolves toxicity), 清熱燥濕 (clears heat and dries damp).

*溫 (wēn)* — warm; the action of warming cold. 溫中 (warms the middle), 溫陽 (warms yang), 溫經 (warms the channels).

*散 (sàn)* — disperse / disseminate. 散寒 (disperses cold), 散結 (dissolves nodules / accumulations).

*行 (xíng)* — move / circulate. 行氣 (regulates qi), 行血 (activates blood).

*活 (huó)* — invigorate / quicken. 活血化瘀 (invigorates blood and transforms blood stasis).

*疏 (shū)* — soothe / release constraint. 疏肝 (soothes the Liver), 疏風 (releases wind).

*化 (huà)* — transform. 化痰 (transforms phlegm), 化濕 (resolves damp), 化瘀 (transforms blood stasis).

*降 (jiàng)* — downbear / cause to descend. 降逆 (downbears counterflow), 降火 (descends fire).

*升 (shēng)* — ascend / cause to rise. 升陽 (raises yang), 升舉 (raises and lifts).

*斂 (liǎn)* — restrain / consolidate. 斂汗 (restrains sweating), 斂肺 (consolidates the Lung).

*固 (gù)* — secure / consolidate. 固表 (secures the exterior), 固精 (secures essence).

*潤 (rùn)* — moisten. 潤肺 (moistens the Lung), 潤腸 (moistens the intestines for dry constipation), 潤燥 (moistens dryness).

*燥 (zào)* — dry. 燥濕 (dries damp).

*利 (lì)* — promote / drain. 利水 (drains water / promotes urination), 利濕 (drains damp), 利尿 (promotes urination).

*止 (zhǐ)* — stop. 止血 (stops bleeding), 止咳 (stops cough), 止瀉 (stops diarrhea), 止痛 (stops pain), 止汗 (stops sweating), 止嘔 (stops vomiting).

*發 (fā)* — release / promote outward. 發汗 (promotes sweating), 發散風寒 (releases wind-cold).

*解 (jiě)* — resolve / release. 解表 (releases the exterior), 解毒 (resolves toxicity), 解鬱 (resolves constraint), 解暑 (resolves summer-heat).

*消 (xiāo)* — disperse / dissolve. 消食 (disperses food stagnation), 消積 (disperses accumulation), 消腫 (reduces swelling).

*調 (tiáo)* — regulate / harmonize. 調和 (harmonizes), 調經 (regulates menstruation), 調和諸藥 (harmonizes all medicines — the distinctive action of licorice).

*健 (jiàn)* — strengthen. 健脾 (strengthens Spleen), 健胃 (strengthens Stomach).

*平 (píng)* — calm / level. 平肝 (calms the Liver), 平喘 (calms wheezing).

*安 (ān)* — calm. 安神 (calms the spirit), 安胎 (calms the fetus).

*提 (tí)* — lift / raise. 提神 (refreshes the spirit), 提氣 (raises qi).

*辟 (bì)* — disperse / drive off. 辟穢 (disperses turbid odors), 辟邪 (drives off evil).

*消食化滯 (xiāo shí huà zhì)* — disperses food stagnation; the canonical action of digestive aromatics.

*透疹 (tòu zhěn)* — promotes the eruption of rashes (as in measles — the supportive use, helping the rash come out rather than suppressing it).

*燥濕化痰 (zào shī huà tán)* — dries dampness and transforms phlegm; the action category of the acrid-warm-drying phlegm drugs, on the classical principle that phlegm arises from undrained damp and that the Spleen is its source (*治痰先治脾*, "to treat phlegm, first treat the Spleen"). Chief drug: 半夏; base formula 二陳湯. Distinguish from 滲濕 (leaching damp out through urination) and from 清熱化痰 (transforming *hot* phlegm). See _ban-xia.typ_, _tangerine-peel.typ_.

*痞 (pǐ, "glomus" / "focal distention")* — a *subjective* sensation of stuffed fullness and blockage below the heart, characteristically *without* pain on pressure and without visible distension — which is what distinguishes it from 脹 (distension) and from 結胸 (chest bind). The indication of the *瀉心湯* formula family, which opens with acrid-warm drugs while descending with bitter-cold ones. See _ban-xia.typ_, _licorice-root.typ_.

*滲濕 (shèn shī, "leach out dampness")* — percolates/leaches accumulated dampness out via urination; a finer-grained companion to 利水 (promotes urination generally), the two combining in the compound action 利水滲濕 (lì shuǐ shèn shī) that is the signature action of both 茯苓 (fúlíng) and 薏苡仁 (yìyǐrén) — see _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*除痺 (chú bì)* — dispels Bì-obstruction (damp-type joint pain); see _coix.typ_.

*涼血消斑 (liáng xuè xiāo bān)* — cools the blood and disperses maculae; the action category for heat forced into the blood level with macular eruption (see 斑 / 發斑). See _qing-dai.typ_.

*定驚 (dìng jīng)* — settles fright; arrests convulsion and tremor, a Liver-wind action. Often paired as 瀉火定驚 (drains fire and settles fright) for convulsive high fever in children. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*沖服 / 包煎 (chōng fú / bāo jiān)* — two administration devices for drugs that cannot simply be boiled with the rest. *沖服* — the powder is *stirred into* the strained, finished decoction and swallowed with it, used for insoluble, precious, or heat-labile drugs. *包煎* — the drug is *wrapped in cloth* before decocting, used for powders, fine hairs, and mucilaginous drugs that would cloud or scorch. 青黛, being water-insoluble, is given 入丸散 (in pills and powders) or 沖服 and *is not decocted*. Related: *另煎* (decocted separately), *先煎* (pre-decocted), *後下* (added late). See _qing-dai.typ_.

*排膿 (pái nóng)* — expels pus; typically paired with 清熱 as 清熱排膿 (clears heat and expels pus), the signature action of raw 薏苡仁 in Lung- and intestinal-abscess formulas. See _coix.typ_.

=== Drug-form vocabulary

*生 (shēng) vs. 熟 (shú), 炙 (zhì), 炮 (páo), 炒 (chǎo), 煨 (wēi), 蒸 (zhēng), 烤 (kǎo)* — the saṃskāra-class processing modifiers. *生* (raw); *熟* (cooked / prepared); *炙* (honey-fried, sometimes wine-fried); *炮* (blast-fried, charred); *炒* (stir-fried); *煨* (roasted in ashes); *蒸* (steamed); *烤* (baked). Each modifier yields a distinct drug-form. See _ginger.typ_, _chinese-licorice.typ_, _rehmannia.typ_ for the foundational examples.

*白礬 / 石灰 / 竹瀝 (báifán / shíhuī / zhúlì)* — three of the corrective agents used in 炮製 processing, named here because the *agent* is what distinguishes one processed form of a drug from another. *白礬* (alum) and *石灰* (lime) are used to reduce the acridity and toxicity of harsh drugs; *竹瀝* (bamboo sap) is itself a cold phlegm-clearing drug, so processing with it can *invert* a warm drug's thermal nature. The pinellia forms 清半夏 (alum), 法半夏 (licorice and lime) and 竹瀝半夏 (bamboo sap) are the clearest illustration. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*九蒸九晒 (jiǔ zhēng jiǔ shài, "nine steamings and nine sun-dryings")* — the elaborated steaming-and-drying process used principally for 熟地黃 rehmannia preparation; among the most-developed saṃskāra procedures in Chinese pharmacy.

=== Formula construction

*君臣佐使 (jūn chén zuǒ shǐ, "sovereign, minister, assistant, envoy")* — the *hierarchical role system of Chinese formula composition*, borrowed from the imagery of a royal court. *君 (jūn,* sovereign) — the chief drug, addressing the principal pattern; *臣 (chén,* minister) — reinforces the sovereign or treats a major concurrent aspect; *佐 (zuǒ,* assistant) — supports, or moderates the sovereign's harshness, or treats a secondary symptom; *使 (shǐ,* envoy) — guides the formula to its target channel and harmonizes the whole (licorice's classic position). Role assignment is *interpretive*: commentators frequently differ on a given formula. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*基礎方 (jīchǔ fāng, "base formula")* — a formula valued less for its own prescription than as the *structural parent* of a family built on it by addition. 四君子湯 (qi) and 四物湯 (blood) are the two canonical examples; to learn a base formula is to learn the grammar of its category. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*加減 (jiā jiǎn, "additions and subtractions")* — the modification of a classical formula to fit the individual presentation, by adding or removing drugs. The practice that makes the classical formulary a grammar rather than a fixed drug list; a named derived formula is a modification that became standard. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*補血而不滯血 (bǔ xuè ér bù zhì xuè, "tonify blood without stagnating blood")* — the companion architectural principle to 補中有瀉, and the design rule of *四物湯*: because blood tonics are rich and static, a blood-supplementing formula must build in *movement* (當歸, 川芎) and *restraint* (白芍) or it becomes a cloying mass the patient cannot digest. The two base formulas make the same structural point about different organs — the Spleen generates damp, so supplement with drainage; blood tonics are heavy, so supplement with movement. See _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*閉門留寇 (bì mén liú kòu, "shutting the door and leaving the bandit inside")* — the classical idiom for *tonifying while a pathogen is unresolved*: supplementation closes the exterior with the pathogen still within, so an acute illness drags on instead of resolving. The cardinal rule against giving tonic formulas during exterior or excess patterns; cf. *補其實 (bǔ qí shí,* "supplementing what is already full"). See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_, _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*滋膩 (zī nì, "rich and cloying")* — the property of sweet, heavy, moistening tonics (熟地黃 above all) that makes them hard for a weak Spleen to transform; the formula-level liability behind the commonest misapplication of blood tonics, producing distension, appetite loss and a greasy tongue coat. Classically corrected by pairing with aromatic qi-movers — *砂仁*, *陳皮*, ginger. The fuller phrase *滋膩礙胃* ("cloying and obstructing the stomach") names the consequence. See _four-substances-decoction.typ_, _rehmannia.typ_.

*補中有瀉 (bǔ zhōng yǒu xiè, "drainage within supplementation")* — the architectural principle that a supplementing formula should build in a draining element, so that tonification does not become stagnation. The teaching examples are *四君子湯* (茯苓 draining among three sweet tonics) and *六味地黃丸* (the 三補三瀉, three tonics balanced by three drainers). See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_, _fuling.typ_, _rehmannia.typ_.

*另煎 (lìng jiān, "decocted separately")* — preparing one drug apart from the rest and combining the liquids, used for costly drugs (ginseng) and for those whose actives do not survive a long shared boil. Related: *先煎* (pre-decocted, for minerals and shells), *後下* (added late, for volatile aromatics). See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_, _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*錢 (qián)* — the classical Chinese weight unit of pre-modern prescriptions, approximately *3.7 g* in late-imperial measure (with real regional and period variation — conversions should be treated as approximate). Classical dose instructions in 錢 are often strikingly small by modern decoction standards, because the delivery was typically a *powder decocted*, not a large raw-herb brew. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*顆粒 / 科學中藥 (kēlì / kēxué zhōngyào, "granules" / "scientific Chinese medicine")* — modern spray-dried concentrated-extract granules, the dominant delivery form in Taiwan, Japan and increasingly elsewhere. *Concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are not interchangeable gram-for-gram with raw-herb decoction doses.* See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

=== Formula classes

*湯 (tāng)* — decoction. The classical preparation: drugs simmered in water and the strained liquid drunk. Most flexible form; the basis of nearly all classical-formula references.

*散 (sàn)* — powder. Drugs ground to fine powder; taken with warm water or wine.

*丸 (wán)* — pill. Drugs ground and bound with honey (蜜丸), water, or paste; the foundational pill form. Many classical pills.

*膏 (gāo)* — paste / extract / ointment. _Inner gāo_ (内膏): concentrated decoction-to-paste form; _outer gāo_ (外膏 / 膏摩 / 膏藥): topical plaster.

*丹 (dān)* — elixir / refined preparation. Originally an alchemical term; now used for highly refined pill or powder preparations.

*煎 (jiān)* — decoction (alternative to 湯; sometimes specifically a long-simmered decoction).

*露 (lù)* — distillate; hydrosol.

*酒 (jiǔ)* — wine; a class of alcoholic-extraction preparations.

*醋 (cù)* — vinegar; both an ingredient and a processing medium for some drugs.

*炮製 (páo zhì)* — drug processing; the general term for the saṃskāra-modifying procedures.

*老火湯 (lǎo huǒ tāng, "old-fire soup")* — the Cantonese tradition of *long-simmered medicinal soups* (two to four hours or more), in which herb-and-meat combinations are cooked down into a daily dietetic preparation; the principal vehicle by which 藥食同源 drugs such as 茯苓, 薏苡仁, 山藥, and 蓮子 are actually consumed in southern China. See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*糖水 (táng shuǐ, "sugar water")* — the Cantonese and Hong Kong class of *sweet dessert soups*, many of them explicitly dietetic (mung bean for heat, Job's tears for damp, black sesame for the Kidney). The dessert-and-medicine overlap is the 藥食同源 principle in its most everyday form. See _coix.typ_, _mung-dhal.typ_.

*涼茶 (liáng chá, "cooling tea")* — the southern-Chinese (especially Cantonese) class of *cooling herbal decoctions* drunk to clear heat and damp in a hot-humid climate, sold in dedicated herbal-tea shops and, now, in bottles. Chrysanthemum, honeysuckle, and _Prunella_ are characteristic ingredients; the register in which 上火 is treated. See _chrysanthemum.typ_, _climates.typ_.

*四神湯 (sì shén tāng, "four spirits soup")* — the Fujianese-Taiwanese household soup of four gentle Spleen-supporting, damp-draining ingredients — *茯苓* (or *芡實*, Euryale seed), *蓮子* (lotus seed), *山藥* (Chinese yam), and *薏苡仁* (Job's tears) — usually simmered with pork. One of the clearest living instances of a formula-logic dish: the combination builds the 補中有瀉 (tonify-with-drainage) balance into a food. See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*工夫茶 (gōng fu chá, "kung-fu tea")* — the small-cup, short-steep, high-leaf-ratio brewing tradition of Fujian / Guangdong / Taiwan; canonical for oolong and aged tea preparations.

*窨花 (yìn huā, "scenting")* — the layering-and-resting process for scented teas (jasmine tea, osmanthus tea). See _jasmine-tea.typ_.

=== Anatomical / physiological terms

*命門 (mìng mén, "gate of life")* — the source of constitutional vitality, located between the kidneys. Foundational to the 補腎 (Kidney-tonifying) clinical category.

*經絡 (jīng luò, "channels and collaterals")* — the twelve principal channels (jīng) and the network of collaterals (luò) through which qi and blood circulate. The acupuncture meridian system.

*奇經八脈 (qí jīng bā mài, "eight extraordinary channels")* — the eight extra-channel system (Du, Ren, Chong, Dai, Yin/Yang Qiao, Yin/Yang Wei) that complements the twelve principal channels.

*營衛 (yíng wèi)* — nutritive (营) and defensive (衛) qi; the two principal forms of qi in surface-and-internal balance.

*骨蒸 (gǔ zhēng, "bone-steaming heat")* — the classical clinical presentation of yin-vacuity heat with low-grade afternoon fever, night sweats, and a feeling of heat in the bones; the signature of chronic yin-vacuity.

=== Named-disease vocabulary

*消渴 (xiāo kě, "consumption-thirst")* — the classical Chinese diabetic-spectrum syndrome; characterized by excessive thirst, hunger, and urination. Mapped to modern diabetes mellitus broadly.

*中風 (zhòng fēng, "wind-strike")* — stroke / cerebrovascular accident in the classical framework; later extended to severe wind-pattern conditions.

*中滿 (zhōng mǎn, "middle-fullness")* — abdominal distension / fullness; the cardinal contraindication to sweet-cloying tonifying drugs (especially licorice).

*梅核氣 (méi hé qì, "plum-pit qi")* — the classical sensation of a lump in the throat (modern: globus pharyngeus); the canonical 半夏厚朴湯 indication.

*癥瘕 (zhēng jiǎ, "concretions and conglomerations")* — chronic abdominal masses; the indication for asafoetida and other dissolving drugs.

*疝氣 (shàn qì, "hernia-qi")* — cold-pattern hernia; the canonical 小茴香 / fennel indication.

*淋 (lìn)* — urinary disorders (multiple sub-types); modern equivalents include UTI, urinary stones, urethritis.

*痰飲 (tán yǐn, "phlegm and thin-mucus")* — accumulated fluid pathology; foundational to the damp-and-phlegm clinical category.

*瘀血 (yū xuè, "blood stasis")* — pathological blood stagnation; the indication for 活血化瘀 drugs.

*斑 / 發斑 (bān / fā bān, "maculae" / "macular eruption")* — the flat, non-raised, purple-red skin eruptions of heat forced into the blood level; a Wen Bing (warm-disease) category, distinguished from raised 疹 (zhěn, papular rash). *溫毒發斑* — warm-toxin with macular eruption — is the canonical indication for 涼血消斑 drugs. See _qing-dai.typ_, _climates.typ_.

*肝火犯肺 (gān huǒ fàn fèi, "Liver fire assailing the Lung")* — the pattern in which exuberant Liver fire mounts to scorch the Lung: a harsh paroxysmal cough with scanty sticky or *blood-streaked* sputum, rib-side pain, irritability, red tongue with yellow coat, wiry rapid pulse. The signature indication of 青黛 and of 黛蛤散. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*驚癇 / 小兒驚風 (jīng xián / xiǎo ér jīng fēng, "fright epilepsy" / "childhood fright-wind")* — the classical category of convulsive disorders in children, typically with high fever; a Liver-channel pathology, the Liver governing wind. The indication of 定驚 (fright-settling) drugs. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*血虛 (xuè xū, "blood vacuity")* — insufficiency of blood, showing in the three registers blood governs: *moistening* (lustreless complexion, dry skin, brittle nails, dry hair), *anchoring the spirit* (palpitations, insomnia, poor memory), and *reaching the periphery* (numbness, tingling, blurred vision, floaters) — plus scanty or delayed menstruation. Tongue *pale, thin, dry*; pulse *thin* (細). *Not interchangeable with anaemia*: the two correlate loosely and are routinely equated in both directions, wrongly. The base formula is *四物湯*. See _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*肝鬱 / 肝氣鬱結 (gān yù / gān qì yù jié, "Liver qi constraint")* — qi stagnation in the Liver's coursing function: rib-side and chest distension, sighing, irritability, premenstrual aggravation, symptoms that *fluctuate with mood*, and a *wiry* (弦) pulse. Via *肝鬱乘脾* ("constrained Liver overwhelming the Spleen") it produces poor appetite and loose stools and so *mimics Spleen-qi vacuity* — one of the commonest diagnostic confusions, with the wiry-versus-weak pulse as the decider. Treated by coursing (逍遙散, 四逆散), not tonifying. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*臟躁 (zàng zào, "restless organ disorder")* — the classical anxious-distressed pattern of women, mapped to modern depression / hysteria-spectrum; the indication for 甘麥大棗湯.

*痺 (bì)* — obstruction-pattern joint disease; modern rheumatoid arthritis and similar. *濕痺 (shī bì)* — the damp sub-type, with heaviness and swelling; the canonical 薏苡仁 indication (see _coix.typ_).

*瘴氣 (zhàng qì, "miasmal qi")* — the classical term for the *damp-heat miasmal illness of hot southern regions* (Lingnan, Jiaozhi, and the far south generally), covering what modern medicine would distribute across malaria, dysentery, and other tropical febrile disease. Historically the dominant medical anxiety of northerners campaigning or posted south; the setting of the Ma Yuan / 薏苡明珠 episode. See _coix.typ_, _climates.typ_.

*上火 (shàng huǒ, "rising fire")* — the living folk-dietetic complaint of *heatiness*: sore throat, mouth ulcers, red eyes, nosebleed, constipation, irritability, attributed to heating foods, weather, or overwork. Not a classical textual diagnosis but one of the most-used everyday health categories in Chinese households, and the register in which chrysanthemum tea, 涼茶, and similar cooling drinks are reached for. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

=== Classical source-texts

*神農本草經 (Shén Nóng Běn Cǎo Jīng)* — the earliest Chinese materia medica, attributed legendarily to Shen Nong; assembled in the Han period (1st–2nd c.); the foundational pharmacology classic. Drugs classed in three grades (superior 上品, middle 中品, inferior 下品).

*本草綱目 (Běn Cǎo Gāng Mù)* — Li Shizhen's _Compendium of Materia Medica_, 1578; the foundational late-imperial pharmacopoeia, ~1,900 entries; the principal pre-modern reference.

*傷寒論 (Shāng Hán Lùn)* — Zhang Zhongjing's _Discussion of Cold Damage_, late Han (~200 CE); the foundational clinical formulary, especially for acute and cold-pattern disease. Many formulas cited throughout.

*金匱要略 (Jīn Guì Yào Lüè)* — Zhang Zhongjing's _Essential Prescriptions from the Golden Cabinet_; the companion to the Shanghan Lun, focused on chronic and miscellaneous diseases.

*太平惠民和劑局方 (Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng)* — the Song-period official formulary (1108).

*脾胃論 (Pí Wèi Lùn)* — Li Dongyuan's _Treatise on the Spleen and Stomach_, Jin-Yuan period (13th c.); foundational to Spleen-Stomach pharmacology.

*溫病條辨 (Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn)* — Wu Jutong's _Systematic Differentiation of Warm Disease_, 1798; foundational to the Wen Bing (warm-disease) school.

*景岳全書 (Jǐng Yuè Quán Shū)* — Zhang Jingyue's _Complete Works of Zhang Jingyue_, 1624.

*小兒藥證直訣 (Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué)* — Qian Yi's _Key to Therapeutics of Children's Diseases_, 1119; the source of 六味地黃丸.

*喫茶養生記 (Kissa Yōjōki, "Notes on Drinking Tea for Nourishing Life")* — Eisai, 1211; the foundational Japanese tea-as-medicine text.

*茶經 (Chá Jīng)* — Lu Yu's _Classic of Tea_, c. 760 CE; the foundational Chinese tea-pharmacy text.

=== Selected drug-names and substance-specific terms

*大蒜 (dàsuàn, "big garlic")* — the canonical Chinese name for garlic (_Allium sativum_); 蒜 (suàn) alone for the generic Allium. Introduced to China from the Western Regions (西域) during the Han period via the Zhang Qian (張騫) embassy. Sub-classifications:
- *獨蒜 / 獨頭蒜 (dú suàn / dú tóu suàn, "single-headed garlic")* — single-clove cultivar; treated as distinctively warming in folk dietetics.
- *青蒜 (qīng suàn, "green garlic")* — fresh young garlic stalks.
- *蒜苗 / 蒜薹 (suànmiáo / suàntái)* — garlic scapes / shoots.
- *黑蒜 (hēi suàn, "black garlic")* — modern (20th-c. Korean/Japanese) aged-fermented black garlic; post-classical.
- *蒜泥 (suàn ní)* — garlic paste, topical.
- *糖醋蒜 / 醋蒜 (táng cù suàn / cù suàn)* — vinegar-pickled garlic; Chinese functional-food preparation.
See _garlic.typ_.

*獨蒜灸 / 隔蒜灸 (dú suàn jiǔ / gé suàn jiǔ, "single-clove moxibustion" / "moxa-on-garlic")* — the classical Chinese moxibustion-through-garlic technique: a slice of garlic placed on the skin with moxa cone burned on top; for chronic abscesses, scrofula, and Vāta-equivalent stagnation. Modern acupuncture-clinical use.

*西域 (Xī Yù, "Western Regions")* — the Han-period Chinese designation for Central Asia and beyond (Persia, Bactria, Sogdiana); the source of many Han-period agricultural introductions including garlic, alfalfa, sesame, cucumber, walnut, and grapes. Documented in _Shi Ji_ (史記) and _Han Shu_ (漢書) via the Zhang Qian (張騫, d. 113 BCE) embassy.

*胡 prefix (hú)* — Chinese-pharmacology marker prefix denoting Iranian / Central-Asian / Silk-Road provenance (cf. 胡桃 walnut, 胡椒 pepper, 胡麻 sesame in classical usage, 胡荽 coriander). The garlic 大蒜 is recorded as 胡蒜 (húsuàn) in some Han-and-Tang sources to mark the Western-Regions origin; the modern 大蒜 naming has displaced the 胡 prefix while preserving the substantive history.

*薄荷 (bòhe)* — the canonical Chinese mint species (_Mentha haplocalyx_); in _Bencao Gangmu_. Distinct from European pennyroyal (poejo / _M. pulegium_); see _poejo.typ_ for the species disambiguation.

*莧 / 莧菜 (xiàn / xiàncài)* — Chinese amaranth leaves (_A. tricolor_ and related species); classical _Bencao_ vegetable. The grain form (籽粒莧 _zǐlì xiàn_) is post-Columbian. See _amaranth.typ_.

*韭菜 (jiǔcài)* — Chinese chive (_Allium tuberosum_). *蔥 (cōng)* — Welsh onion / scallion (_A. fistulosum_). *薤 (xiè) / 蕗蕎 (lùqiáo)* — Chinese onion (_A. chinense_).

*苦艾 / 洋艾 (kǔ'ài / yáng'ài, "bitter mugwort" / "foreign mugwort")* — the modern Chinese names for European wormwood (_Artemisia absinthium_); 洋 marks the Western introduction. *Not* a classical Bencao drug — distinguish sharply from the canonical Artemisia species below. See _wormwood.typ_.

*艾葉 / 青蒿 / 茵陳 (àiyè / qīnghāo / yīnchén)* — the three canonical classical Artemisia drugs, all *distinct species* from European wormwood: *艾葉* (mugwort leaf, _A. argyi_), the moxibustion (灸) herb, warms channels and stops bleeding; *青蒿* (sweet wormwood, _A. annua_), clears summerheat and 截瘧 (checks malaria) — the source of artemisinin, via Ge Hong's _Zhǒuhòu Bèijí Fāng_ (肘後備急方); *茵陳(蒿)* (_A. capillaris / A. scoparia_), the premier drug for damp-heat 黃疸 (jaundice), in 茵陳蒿湯. See _wormwood.typ_ for the genus disambiguation.

*丁香 (dīngxiāng, "nail aromatic")* — clove (_Syzygium aromaticum_); the warming interior drug for stomach-cold hiccup and vomiting (溫中降逆) and Kidney-yang cold (溫腎助陽). Drug-forms: *公丁香 (gōng dīngxiāng, "male clove")* = the flower bud (principal drug); *母丁香 (mǔ dīngxiāng, "mother clove")* = the milder ripe fruit; the old Han-court name *雞舌香 (jīshéxiāng, "chicken-tongue aromatic")* records its breath-freshening use. Classically 畏 (fears) 郁金 among the 十九畏. The 丁 "nail" naming parallels Latin _clāvus_ and Persian _mīkhak_. See _cloves.typ_.

*胡桃 / 核桃 (hútáo / hétáo)* — walnut (_Juglans regia_); kernel drug *核桃仁 / 胡桃仁 (hétáo rén)*, warm-sweet, tonifies Kidney yang, warms the Lung (定喘), moistens the intestine — the "brain-shaped nut benefits the brain" signature. The 胡 prefix marks Silk-Road introduction (cf. the 胡-prefix glossary note). The American black walnut *黑胡桃 (hēi hútáo, _Juglans nigra_)* is a modern introduction with no classical entry — its green-hull drug is unrelated to the tonic kernel. See _black-walnut.typ_.

*葡萄 / 葡萄乾 (pútáo / pútáo gān)* — grape / dried grape (raisin) (_Vitis vinifera_); sweet-sour, neutral, a qi-blood and Liver-Kidney tonic in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ superior class (上品). 葡萄 is a Han loanword accompanying the Silk-Road introduction of the vine (cf. 西域). See _raisins.typ_.

*枸杞子 / 地骨皮 (gǒuqǐzǐ / dìgǔpí)* — the two *distinct* drugs of _Lycium barbarum_/_chinense_: *枸杞子* the fruit (goji berry), sweet-neutral, tonifies Liver-Kidney yin/essence and *brightens the eyes* (益精明目); *地骨皮 ("earth-bone bark")* the root-bark, sweet-bland-cold, clears deficiency (empty) heat and cools blood (for 骨蒸潮熱, steaming-bone fever). Premier region: *寧夏枸杞 (Níngxià gǒuqǐ)*. See _goji-berry.typ_.

*六大茶類 (liù dà chá lèi, "six great tea categories")* — the standard classification of _Camellia sinensis_ teas by processing/oxidation: 綠茶 (green, unoxidized), 白茶 (white, minimally processed), 黃茶 (yellow), 青茶/烏龍 (oolong, partial), 紅茶 (red/"black," full), 黑茶 (dark/post-fermented, e.g. pu-erh). All are one species; _saṃskāra_ (processing) makes the drug. See _green-tea.typ_, _oolong-tea.typ_, _white-tea.typ_, _puerh-tea.typ_, _red-tea.typ_.

*白茶 (báichá, "white tea")* — the minimally-processed tea category (withered and dried only, no kill-green, no rolling); "white" names the silvery bud-down, not the liquor. Grades by pluck: *白毫銀針 (Bái Háo Yín Zhēn,* Silver Needle, buds only), *白牡丹 (Bái Mǔdān,* White Peony), *貢眉 / 壽眉 (Gòng Méi / Shòu Méi,* mature leaf). Made in *福鼎 / 政和 (Fúdǐng / Zhènghé)*, Fujian; aged form *老白茶 (lǎo báichá)*, valued per the saying *一年茶、三年藥、七年寶* ("one year tea, three years medicine, seven years treasure"). See _white-tea.typ_.

*萎凋 (wěidiāo, "withering")* — the slow moisture-loss step (sun-and-shade or indoor) that opens tea processing; in white tea it is essentially the *whole* process, and the absence of a subsequent 殺青 (kill-green) allows the slow, light enzymatic oxidation that distinguishes white from green. See _white-tea.typ_.

*白毫 (báiháo, "white down")* — the fine silvery trichomes on the unopened tea bud; the source, via Amoy/Hokkien _pe̍h-ho_, of the English tea-grading term *pekoe* (as in "orange pekoe"). See _white-tea.typ_.

*丹参 / 鼠尾草 (dānshēn / shǔwěicǎo)* — the two Chinese _Salvia_ referents, *sharply distinct*: *丹参 ("cinnabar root")* is the *root* of _Salvia miltiorrhiza_, a classical superior-class blood-invigorating drug (活血化瘀, for chest _bì_/angina and blood stasis), used in 丹参飲; *鼠尾草 ("rat-tail herb")* is the *modern, non-classical* botanical name for European garden sage (_S. officinalis_), the leaf-aromatic of the West. Same genus, different species, part (root vs. leaf), and action — the Western "salvia/sage" is *not* danshen. See _sage.typ_.

*蘋果 / 苹果 (píngguǒ)* — the modern cultivated apple (_Malus domestica_); a *late Western introduction* (西洋蘋果, 19th–20th c.), *not* a classical _Bencao_ drug; the name is from Buddhist 頻婆果 (_pínpó_, a Sanskrit transliteration). The *classical* Chinese pomes are the native crabapples *林檎 (línqín, "forest fowl," _M. asiatica_/_prunifolia_)* and *柰 (nài)* — sweet-sour, thirst-quenching (止渴), astringent (澀) for diarrhea — which have _Bencao_ entries. See _apple.typ_.

*梨 (lí)* — pear (_Pyrus_ spp.); a *deeply classical* fruit-drug: cool, sweet, Lung-and-Stomach, the paradigmatic *清熱潤肺* (clear-heat, moisten-Lung), *生津止渴*, and *化痰止咳* fruit for dry cough and autumn dryness (秋燥). Cultivar-names *雪梨 (snow pear), 鴨梨 (duck pear), 沙梨 (sand pear, _P. pyrifolia_)*; the classic preparations *秋梨膏 (qiūlígāo,* autumn-pear cough syrup), *川貝燉梨* (pear stewed with Fritillaria), *冰糖雪梨* (rock-sugar snow-pear). See _pear.typ_.

*人參 / 人参 (rénshēn)* — ginseng (_Panax ginseng_), the root; _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品 and the *archetype of the 補氣 category*. Sweet and slightly bitter, slightly warm (微溫), entering Spleen, Lung, Heart (and Kidney in modern sources); the drug of 大補元氣, 補脾益肺, 生津止渴, and 安神益智. The name is *人* ("human") + 參, recording the *anthropomorphic forked root* — a classical doctrine-of-signatures grading criterion, not a pharmacological variable. Classically *人參反藜蘆* (十八反) and *人參畏五靈脂* (十九畏); the *蘆頭* (rhizome neck) is removed as emetic. *Chinese drug-forms:* *生曬參* (sun-dried), *紅參* (steamed = red, warmer and stronger), *糖參 / 白參*, *人參鬚* (fine rootlets, weak and cheap), *野山參* (wild), *林下參* (forest-grown). Premium origin 吉林 / 長白山. See _korean-ginseng.typ_ for the full treatment and the Korean drug-form system.

*參 (shēn) — the root-drug suffix, and a standing disambiguation.* 參 is *not* a word meaning "ginseng": it is a suffix borne by many tonic-and-medicinal roots, several *botanically unrelated* to _Panax_ and to each other. *Same genus, different drugs:* *西洋參 (xīyángshēn,* American ginseng, _P. quinquefolius_ — *cool* and yin-fluid-tonifying, the thermal *opposite* of 人參); *三七 / 田七 (sānqī / tiánqī,* _P. notoginseng_ — a blood-mover and bleeding-stopper). *Different families entirely:* *黨參 (dǎngshēn,* _Codonopsis pilosula_ — the mild, cheap qi-tonic substitute for ginseng, lacking its emergency action); *太子參 (tàizǐshēn,* _Pseudostellaria heterophylla_ — gentle qi-and-yin tonic for children); *丹參 (dānshēn,* _Salvia miltiorrhiza_ root — blood-invigorator, see _sage.typ_); *玄參 (xuánshēn,* _Scrophularia_); *苦參 (kǔshēn,* _Sophora flavescens_ — bitter-cold damp-heat drug). See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*隔水燉 (gé shuǐ dùn, "double-boiled over water")* — the classical gentle-extraction method for costly tonic drugs: the drug is sealed in a covered vessel set inside a pot of simmering water, so it is never boiled hard and nothing is lost to a shared decoction. The standard preparation for ginseng; *含服* (holding a slice in the mouth to suck) is the other reserved-drug method. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*半夏 (bànxià, "half summer")* — pinellia (_Pinellia ternata_, Araceae), the dried tuber; the premier *燥濕化痰* (damp-drying, phlegm-transforming) and *降逆止嘔* (counterflow-directing, vomiting-arresting) drug. Acrid (辛), warm (溫), *有毒*; Spleen, Stomach, Lung. *神農本草經 下品* — the lower class, i.e. potent, toxic, not for prolonged use. *The name is a calendar marker*: the plant appears at midsummer (*五月半夏生*, _Lǐjì_ "Yuèlìng"), and the Japanese seasonal day *半夏生 (hangeshō)*, ~1–2 July, is named after it and once set the deadline for rice transplanting. Older synonyms: *地文*, *守田* ("field-guardian" — it is a field weed), *水玉*. Classically *反烏頭* (counteracts aconite) among the *十八反* — canonical but contested in practice. *Processed forms, which are the whole drug:* *生半夏* raw and *toxic*, external use only; *清半夏* (alum) for phlegm-damp; *姜半夏* (ginger and alum) for vomiting; *法半夏* (licorice and lime), gentler; *半夏曲* (fermented) for phlegm with food stagnation; *竹瀝半夏* (bamboo sap), which *inverts* the drug's warmth for use in hot phlegm. "半夏" unqualified is an incomplete specification. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*花生 / 落花生 (huāshēng / luòhuāshēng)* — *peanut* (_Arachis hypogaea_). The full name means "fallen-flower produce" and is a *botanical observation*: the flower drops and the pod forms *underground* (geocarpy). Sweet (甘), neutral (平); Spleen, Lung; *健脾養胃*, *潤肺化痰*, *通乳*. Also *長生果 (chángshēngguǒ,* "longevity fruit"), a folk-auspicious name. *Post-Columbian and late* — 16th-c. introduction, recorded in the Qing supplement _Běncǎo Gāngmù Shíyí_ (1765), absent from the Han canon. *花生衣 / 花生紅衣 (huāshēng yī,* "peanut coat") — *the thin red seed-coat, a separate drug*, used to *止血* (stop bleeding) and in a modern Chinese clinical register for thrombocytopenia; it is also the part removed by blanching and absent from most peanut butter. See _peanut.typ_.

*芝麻醬 (zhīmajiàng)* — Chinese sesame paste: *unhulled*, hard-roasted, stone-ground thick and dark; *not* pourable, and let down with water by the *澥 (xiè)* technique, in which the paste first seizes before loosening. *醬* properly denotes a *fermented* paste; sesame paste is not fermented, the character being used loosely for thick pastes. *Distinct from Levantine ṭaḥīna* (hulled, light-roasted, pourable) and Japanese *練り胡麻*. See _sesame-seeds.typ_.

*青黛 (qīng dài)* — *indigo naturalis*: the blue-black pigment precipitated from indigo-bearing leaves by lime-water fermentation and skimmed as froth — a *processed precipitate*, not a plant part. Salty (鹹), cold (寒); Liver, Lung, Stomach; *清熱解毒*, *涼血消斑*, *清肝瀉火*, *定驚*. *The name is cosmetic in origin:* 黛 is not a general colour word but the *blue-black pigment with which women painted their eyebrows* (黛眉; 粉黛, "powder and brow-black," a metonym for women), so 青黛 reads "blue-black brow-pigment." A Tang–Song entrant, *not* in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_. *Water-insoluble and therefore never decocted* — see 沖服 / 包煎. Source plants: 馬藍 (_Baphicacanthus cusia_), 蓼藍 (_Polygonum tinctorium_), 菘藍 (_Isatis indigotica_). Related names: *靛花 (diànhuā,* "indigo flower," the skimmed froth and the true drug), *藍靛*. See _qing-dai.typ_, _ban-lan-gen.typ_.

*藍 / 靛 (lán / diàn)* — *藍* the indigo plant and the colour blue (one of the oldest plant-and-colour words in Chinese; cf. the Xunzi proverb *青出於藍而勝於藍*); *靛* the extracted indigo dye itself. The 藍-named drug plants: *馬藍* (Acanthaceae), *蓼藍* (Polygonaceae), *菘藍* (Brassicaceae, woad) — three unrelated families converging on the same pigment. The three canonical drugs they yield are *板藍根* (root), *大青葉* (leaf), and *青黛* (pigment). See _ban-lan-gen.typ_, _qing-dai.typ_.

*茯苓 (fúlíng)* — Poria (_Wolfiporia_ / _Poria cocos_), the dried sclerotium of a pine-root fungus; _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品 (superior grade), sweet-and-*bland* (see 淡味滲泄), neutral, and by formula-frequency one of the most-used drugs in the pharmacopoeia — classically 平和 ("harmonious," rarely contraindicated). Early sources also write *伏靈 / 伏苓 (fúlíng, "crouching numen")*, reflecting the classical folk account that the sclerotium is the pine's vital essence congealed underground; Li Shizhen collates it in the _Bencao Gangmu_, and the lore pairs *菟絲* (dodder) visible above with 伏靈 beneath. *Drug-form system* (part-position parallels clinical target):
- *茯苓个 (fúlíng gè)* — the whole undivided sclerotium.
- *白茯苓 / 茯苓塊 (bái fúlíng / fúlíng kuài)* — white inner flesh; the standard drug, strongest on 健脾.
- *赤茯苓 (chì fúlíng)* — the pinkish layer beneath the rind; more draining, less tonifying; for damp-heat.
- *茯苓皮 (fúlíng pí)* — the outer rind, for *superficial* edema (皮水, "skin-water") — the skin of the drug for the water in the skin.
- *茯神 (fúshén, "poria-spirit")* — the portion grown around an enclosed pine root, reserved for 寧心安神 (calming the Heart, settling the spirit).
- *茯神木 (fúshén mù)* — the embedded pine-root core itself; a minor drug for Liver-wind and limb spasm.
- *朱茯神 / 朱砂拌茯神* — historically 茯神 dusted with *cinnabar (朱砂, mercuric sulfide)*. *A mercury preparation: not to be used*; restricted or prohibited in modern practice. Recorded for textual completeness only.
See _fuling.typ_.

*猪苓 / 豬苓 (zhūlíng)* — _Polyporus umbellatus_, another sclerotium-forming polypore and the textually fixed *contrast drug* to 茯苓: zhūlíng *drains water more strongly but does not tonify*, while fúlíng drains gently and simultaneously supports the Spleen. The pair appears together in 五苓散 and 豬苓湯 and is the standard teaching example of "draining with support" versus "draining alone." See _fuling.typ_.

*鯽魚 (jìyú)* — crucian carp; a classical _Bencao_ freshwater-fish drug, sweet and neutral, entering Spleen and Stomach, used for 健脾利水 and, canonically, as *鯽魚湯 (jìyú tāng,* crucian-carp soup) — the best-known Chinese *postpartum lactation-and-recovery* food-drug (通乳). Modern households routinely substitute tilapia into this slot, which is how an introduced food acquires traditional standing — observed practice, not classical text. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*罗非鱼 / 羅非魚 (luófēiyú)* — tilapia; a *phonetic transliteration*, and a 20th-century introduction (_O. mossambicus_ 1957, _O. niloticus_ 1978) with *no Bencao entry*; also marketed as *福寿鱼 (fúshòuyú,* "fortune-and-longevity fish"). See _tilapia.typ_.

*菊 / 菊花 (jú / júhuā)* — chrysanthemum flower (_Chrysanthemum × morifolium_); archaic form *鞠 (jú)*, as in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (上品). *野菊花 (yějúhuā)* — wild chrysanthemum (_C. indicum_), a separate, more bitter and cold heat-toxin-clearing drug. Premium cultivated grades: *杭菊 (Hángjú)*, *滁菊 (Chújú)*, *亳菊 (Bójú)*, *貢菊 (Gòngjú,* "tribute chrysanthemum"). *菊花酒 (júhuājiǔ)* — chrysanthemum wine, the Double Ninth Festival (*重陽節, Chóngyáng Jié*) seasonal preparation. *Caution*: *not* to be confused with European chamomile, rendered in modern Chinese as *洋甘菊 (yáng gānjú, "foreign sweet-chrysanthemum")* — the 洋-prefix marks the Western import, parallel to 洋艾/苦艾 for wormwood (above). Nor with the unrelated cultural trope *四君子 (sì jūnzǐ, "Four Gentlemen")* — plum, orchid, bamboo, and chrysanthemum in literati painting/poetry — which shares its name by coincidence with, but is entirely distinct from, the formula *四君子湯 (Sì Jūnzǐ Tāng)* below. See _chrysanthemum.typ_, _chamomile.typ_.

*四君子湯 (Sì Jūnzǐ Tāng, "Four Gentlemen Decoction")* — the *foundational Spleen-qi-tonifying formula* and the 基礎方 of the whole 補氣 category: *人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草* in equal parts, from the Song imperial _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (1107–1110). The name turns on *君子*, the Confucian "gentleman" who governs by virtue rather than force — four mild, even-tempered (平和) tonics — with a pun on 君 as the technical term for a formula's *sovereign* drug. Its derived family: *異功散* (+陳皮), *六君子湯* (+陳皮, 半夏), *香砂六君子湯*, *參苓白朮散*, *八珍湯* (with 四物湯), *十全大補湯*. *Disambiguate* from the painting/poetry 四君子 trope above, with which it shares only the metaphor. Full treatment: _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*四物湯 (Sì Wù Tāng, "Four Substances Decoction")* — the foundational *blood*-nourishing formula and 基礎方 of the blood-tonifying category: *熟地黃, 當歸, 白芍, 川芎*, from the Song _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (1107–1110) — though the combination appears earlier, in the Tang *仙授理傷續斷秘方*, as a *trauma* formula, repurposed from moving damaged blood to nourishing deficient blood. The name is deliberately plain (*物*, "thing, substance"), in pointed contrast to 四君子湯's Confucian conceit. Design rule: *補血而不滯血*. Derived family: *桃紅四物湯* (+桃仁, 紅花, for stasis), *膠艾湯* (+阿膠, 艾葉, for bleeding), *八珍湯* (with 四君子湯), *十全大補湯*, *聖愈湯*, *溫經湯*. Full treatment: _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*白芍 / 赤芍 (bái sháo / chì sháo, white and red peony)* — *two distinct drugs, not grades*, both from _Paeonia_ roots. *白芍* — bitter, sour, slightly cold; Liver, Spleen; *養血斂陰* (nourishes blood, astringes yin), *柔肝止痛* (softens the Liver, relieves pain), *平抑肝陽*; the nourishing-and-holding peony of 四物湯 and 逍遙散. *赤芍* — bitter, slightly cold; Liver; *清熱涼血*, *散瘀止痛*; cooling and blood-*moving*, with no tonifying action. Substituting one for the other changes a formula's direction: *white nourishes and holds; red cools and moves.* Classically 反藜蘆 (among the 十八反). *No substance entry yet.* See _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*川芎 (chuān xiōng)* — the rhizome of _Ligusticum chuanxiong_; pungent, warm; Liver, Gallbladder, Pericardium; *活血行氣* (invigorates blood and moves qi), *祛風止痛*. Classically *血中之氣藥 (xuè zhōng zhī qì yào,* "the qi-medicine within the blood") — it moves qi *within* the blood aspect, which is why a few grams of it keep a formula of rich blood tonics from becoming static. Acrid, warm and *ascending*, so it carries a formula to the head and exterior (hence blood-vacuity headache, and the dermatological maxim *治風先治血，血行風自滅* — "treat wind by first treating blood; when blood moves, wind is extinguished") — and so is also the ingredient most likely to aggravate rising heat, headache, or bleeding when the pattern is wrong. *No substance entry yet.* See _four-substances-decoction.typ_.

*理中丸 (Lǐ Zhōng Wán, "Regulate the Middle Pill")* — 人參, 白朮, 炙甘草, 乾薑; for Spleen-Stomach *vacuity cold* (cold limbs, watery diarrhoea, abdominal pain relieved by warmth). Structurally 四君子湯 with 乾薑 in place of 茯苓 — the substitution that distinguishes a *warming* formula from a *damp-draining* one. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_, _ginger.typ_.

*白朮 (báizhú)* — the rhizome of _Atractylodes macrocephala_; bitter-sweet and warm, entering Spleen and Stomach; *健脾益氣* (strengthens Spleen, augments qi), *燥濕利水* (dries damp, promotes urination), *止汗*, *安胎* (calms the fetus). Usually *炒白朮* (stir-fried) when Spleen-strengthening is wanted. *Distinguish from 蒼朮 (cāngzhú,* _A. lancea_), which is *more strongly drying and much less tonifying* — appropriate where damp dominates and vacuity does not; choosing 白朮 over 蒼朮 declares a formula to be about vacuity rather than damp. *No substance entry yet*; see _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_ for the fuller summary.

*炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo, "honey-fried licorice")* — licorice root stir-fried with honey: *warmer and more tonifying* than raw 生甘草, which is cooler and clears heat and toxicity. The form required wherever a formula warms and supplements the middle; substituting raw licorice changes the formula's thermal character, so the processing is part of the composition, not a sourcing detail. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_, _chinese-licorice.typ_, _licorice-root.typ_.

*薏苡仁 / 薏苡 / 薏米 / 薏仁 (yìyǐrén / yìyǐ / yìmǐ / yìrén)* — Job's tears seed (_Coix lacryma-jobi_ var. _ma-yuen_); _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品; sweet-and-*bland* (see 淡味滲泄), cool, entering Spleen, Stomach, and — distinctively — *Lung*, which underwrites its signature 清熱排膿 (heat-clearing, pus-expelling) action in lung and intestinal abscess. Classically noted as *mild and slow*, requiring larger doses (up to 30 g) and time to act; *妊娠慎用* (caution in pregnancy) — a classical caution later supported by modern uterine-stimulant findings. Drug-forms: *生薏苡仁 (shēng yìyǐrén)* raw, for damp-draining and pus-expelling; *炒 / 麩炒薏苡仁 (chǎo / fū-chǎo yìyǐrén)* dry- or bran-fried, for Spleen-strengthening and checking diarrhoea (健脾止瀉).

*薏苡明珠 (yìyǐ míngzhū, "Job's tears [taken for] bright pearls")* — the idiom, still current, for *being falsely accused through malicious misconstruction of an innocent act*; from the Eastern Han general *馬援 (Ma Yuan)*, who brought cartloads of Job's tears north from his southern campaign as a remedy against 瘴氣 and was posthumously slandered as having smuggled looted pearls. _Hòu Hàn Shū_, 馬援列傳. The plant's botanical varietal epithet *ma-yuen* commemorates the same general. See _coix.typ_.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

=== Fundamental concepts

*त्रिदोष / त्रिदोषाः (tridoṣa / tridoṣāḥ, "three doṣas")* — the three constitutional bio-energies that govern the body's function:
- *वात / वातः (vāta)* — air-and-space; governs movement, nervous-system function, breath, circulation, elimination. _Vāta-prakopa_ → degenerative, neurological, joint disorders.
- *पित्त / पित्तः (pitta)* — fire-and-water; governs digestion, metabolism, transformation, vision, intellect. _Pitta-prakopa_ → inflammatory, febrile, bilious disorders.
- *कफ / कफः (kapha)* — earth-and-water; governs structure, lubrication, immunity, sweetness. _Kapha-prakopa_ → respiratory damp-mucus, metabolic, obesity disorders.

*प्रकृति (prakṛti)* — constitutional type at birth; the unique dominance pattern of the three doṣas (vāta, pitta, kapha, or combinations). Seven canonical types: vāta-prakṛti, pitta-prakṛti, kapha-prakṛti, vāta-pitta, pitta-kapha, vāta-kapha, sama-prakṛti (balanced). Analogous to mizāj-mu'tadil in Tibb and to constitutional type broadly.

*विकृति (vikṛti)* — current state of doṣa imbalance; the disease-pattern manifest at examination.

*अग्नि (agni, "fire")* — digestive fire; the body's transformative power. Healthy _agni_ = good digestion / metabolism. _Manda-agni_ (weak), _tīkṣṇa-agni_ (intense), _viṣama-agni_ (irregular), _sama-agni_ (balanced).

*आम (āma)* — undigested-food residue / metabolic-toxic burden produced by weak _agni_. The Ayurvedic source-substance of disease in chronic conditions. _Āma-pācana_ — the digestion / burning of accumulated āma; central to many therapeutic regimens.

*ओजस् / ओजस (ojas)* — vital essence; the refined product of healthy digestion; correlates broadly with immune-and-vitality function. _Ojas-vardhana_ — ojas-promoting (the canonical milk, ghee, honey class).

*प्राण (prāṇa)* — life-breath / vital energy; analogous to qì. Five sub-prāṇas: prāṇa (chest, intake), apāna (pelvis, downward elimination), samāna (umbilicus, digestion), vyāna (whole body, circulation), udāna (throat-head, speech-thought).

*तेजस् (tejas)* — fire / brightness / the refined essence of pitta; one of the three pillars (with prāṇa and ojas).

*सत्त्व, रजस्, तमस् (sattva, rajas, tamas)* — the three guṇas of the mind / cosmos in the broader Sāṅkhya-derived framework. _Sattva_: clarity, harmony. _Rajas_: activity, restlessness. _Tamas_: inertia, dullness. Used in dietetic classifications (sāttvic / rajasic / tamasic foods).

=== Pharmacological framework — the four-and-fifth parameters of a drug

*रस / रसाः (rasa / rasāḥ)* — taste; the foundational pharmacological parameter. Six rasas: मधुर (madhura, sweet), अम्ल (amla, sour), लवण (lavaṇa, salt), कटु (kaṭu, pungent), तिक्त (tikta, bitter), कषाय (kaṣāya, astringent). Each rasa has doṣa effect (e.g. madhura pacifies vāta-pitta, aggravates kapha) and specific clinical actions. Source: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26.

*गुण / गुणाः (guṇa / guṇāḥ)* — quality; the physical attributes of a substance. Classical list of twenty (in pairs of opposites): guru-laghu (heavy-light), snigdha-rūkṣa (unctuous-dry), uṣṇa-śīta (hot-cold, in the _guṇa_ sense — distinct from vīrya), mṛdu-tīkṣṇa (soft-sharp), manda-tīkṣṇa, sthira-cala (stable-mobile), sūkṣma-sthūla (subtle-gross), viśada-picchila (clear-slimy), and so on. The principal pharmacologically-active guṇas vary by drug.

*वीर्य (vīrya, "potency")* — the energetic potency of a drug, on a binary axis: उष्ण (uṣṇa, heating / warming) or शीत (śīta, cooling). Some traditions recognize an eight-vīrya extended set (uṣṇa, śīta, snigdha, rūkṣa, viśada, picchila, mṛdu, tīkṣṇa) — the "eight extended vīryas" — but the two-vīrya hot-cold framework dominates classical references.

*अनुष्णाशीत (anuṣṇāśīta, "neither heating nor cooling")* — the dravyaguna category for *temperate / neutral vīrya*, used where a drug sits on neither side of the uṣṇa-śīta axis. The exact counterpart of the Chinese 平 (píng, neutral) property, and the term this book uses when rendering a neutral Chinese drug onto Ayurvedic axes. See _fuling.typ_.

*अव्यक्त रस / अल्प रस (avyakta rasa / alpa rasa)* — "indistinct" / "scant" taste; the dravyaguna devices for a substance of faint or barely-perceptible flavour. *Note the structural asymmetry they expose:* these describe the *absence* of a flavour, whereas the Chinese 淡 (bland) is a *positive* pharmacological category with its own drainage action (淡味滲泄). The six rasas have no equivalent of "drains because bland." See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*विपाक (vipāka, "post-digestive transformation")* — the post-digestive (post-metabolic) taste; how the drug's rasa transforms after assimilation. Three vipākas: madhura (sweet — for sweet, salty, and sour rasas), amla (sour — though rarely cited), kaṭu (pungent — for pungent, bitter, and astringent rasas). The vipāka is independent of the predigestive rasa: e.g. ginger has _kaṭu rasa_ but _madhura vipāka_ — a counterintuitive feature with constitutional implications.

*प्रभाव (prabhāva, "specific action")* — the action of a drug not predictable from its rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka. The "fifth parameter," reserved for actions that classical commentary cannot derive from the standard four. Source: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. Example: gokṣura's *aśmari-bhedana* (stone-breaking) is at prabhāva level.

*कर्म / कर्माणि (karma / karmāṇi, "action(s)")* — the clinical effects of a drug; the long action-list typically given at the end of a dravyaguna entry.

*दोष-हर / दोष-शामक (doṣa-hara / doṣa-śāmaka)* — doṣa-pacifying. Each drug has doṣa effect: _vāta-hara_ (Vāta-pacifying), _pitta-hara_, _kapha-hara_, _tridoṣa-hara_ (pacifies all three — the highest classification).

=== Action vocabulary (Karma)

*दीपन (dīpana)* — kindling the digestive fire; the action of warming-aromatic carminatives. The canonical action of ginger, cumin, fennel.

*पाचन (pācana)* — digesting accumulated āma; the action of digestion-supporting drugs (often paired with dīpana as _dīpana-pācana_).

*अनुलोमन / वातानुलोमन (anulomana / vāta-anulomana)* — redirecting downward / restoring Vāta's downward flow; relieves gas, bloating, constipation. The canonical action of carminatives and gentle laxatives.

*रोचन (rocana)* — appetite-restoring, relish-giving; the action that makes food palatable (often paired with dīpana-pācana). Canonical of aromatic spices like clove. See _cloves.typ_.

*छर्दिघ्न (chardighna)* — antiemetic; arrests _chardi_ (vomiting/nausea). The signature action of clove (_lavaṅga_), converging with the Chinese 降逆止嘔. See _cloves.typ_.

*मुखशुद्धिकर / मुखदौर्गन्ध्यहर (mukhaśuddhikara / mukhadaurgandhyahara)* — mouth-cleansing / breath-freshening; the action behind the _tāmbūla_ (betel-quid) use of aromatics such as clove. See _cloves.typ_.

*कुष्ठघ्न / कण्डूघ्न (kuṣṭhaghna / kaṇḍūghna)* — antidermatosis (against _kuṣṭha_, the broad class of skin diseases) / antipruritic; the register of astringent-bitter skin drugs, applied to the antifungal use of black-walnut hull. See _black-walnut.typ_.

*भेदन (bhedana)* — purgation / penetration; the action of stronger laxatives.

*मृदु-विरेचन / मृदु-भेदन (mṛdu-virecana / mṛdu-bhedana)* — gentle purgation; the canonical Triphalā action.

*विरेचन (virecana)* — purgation; the strong purgative action, used in pañcakarma.

*वमन (vamana)* — therapeutic emesis; one of the five pañcakarma procedures.

*ग्राही (grāhī)* — binding (stools); the anti-diarrheal astringent action.

*स्तम्भन (stambhana)* — restraining / stopping action; for excessive flow (bleeding, urination, perspiration).

*स्नेहन (snehana)* — oleation / unctuous-treatment; the foundational pre-pañcakarma procedure of saturating with medicated oil or ghee.

*स्वेदन (svedana)* — sweating / fomentation; the second pre-pañcakarma procedure.

*शोधन (śodhana)* — cleansing / purification, in *two distinct senses* that should not be conflated: (1) *therapeutic* — the strong-clearing procedural class applied to the patient (vamana, virecana, etc.); (2) *pharmaceutical* — the *purificatory processing of a drug* before it is fit to use, applied classically to the toxic and acrid dravyas: *vatsanābha* (aconite), *bhallātaka* (marking nut), *sūraṇa*, and the mineral and metallic preparations. The second sense is the Ayurvedic counterpart of the Chinese 炮製 and the frame in which a drug like 半夏 would be classed. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*सूरण (sūraṇa)* — elephant-foot yam (_Amorphophallus paeoniifolius_, Araceae), a classical dravya and the Indian member of the acrid-aroid problem: raw it is *कण्डू-कर (kaṇḍū-kara,* itch- and irritation-producing), and it requires *śodhana* — boiling, or treatment with tamarind or buttermilk, or burial and ageing — before use. Noted because Ayurveda, Chinese medicine, Greco-Arabic pharmacy and European cottage industry *independently* identified the Araceae as acrid-but-valuable and independently arrived at processing as the answer. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*शमन (śamana)* — pacification; the mild non-cleansing therapeutic class.

*रसायन (rasāyana)* — rejuvenative; the class of drugs and procedures aimed at longevity, anti-aging, vitalization. Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1 (the foundational rasāyana chapter). Foundational drugs: aśvagandhā, śatāvarī, āmalakī, yaṣṭimadhu, triphalā, Cyavanaprāśa, Brāhmī.

*वाजीकरण (vājīkaraṇa)* — virilification / aphrodisiac; the class concerned with reproductive vigor. Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 2.

*जीवनीय (jīvanīya, "life-promoting")* — the classical category of *life-giving, vitality-restoring* drugs; Caraka names a *jīvanīya gaṇa*, a group of ten such herbs. Distinguished from _rasāyana_ (rejuvenation over time) by its sense of *sustaining or restoring life itself* — the register into which the 大補元氣 action of ginseng in collapse most naturally translates. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*श्रमहर / क्लमहर (śrama-hara / klama-hara)* — relieving physical fatigue (_śrama_) and lassitude or exhaustion (_klama_); the dravyaguna register of the modern "asthenia and fatigue" indication. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*बल्य (balya)* — strengthening.

*बृंहण (bṛṃhaṇa)* — anabolic / nourishing-and-building.

*लंघन (laṅghana)* — catabolic / reducing-and-clearing.

*मेध्य (medhya)* — intellect-promoting. The medhya rasāyana class: brāhmī, maṇḍūkaparṇī, yaṣṭimadhu, śaṅkhapuṣpī, jyotiṣmatī.

*स्मृति-प्रद (smṛti-prada)* — memory-giving; the specifically mnemonic register of the _medhya_ class (the reasoned reading of sage's classical memory reputation). See _sage.typ_.

*कण्ठ्य / स्वर्य (kaṇṭhya / svarya)* — throat-benefiting / voice-improving; the register of demulcent-and-astringent throat drugs (yaṣṭimadhu, and by extension sage). See _sage.typ_, _licorice-root.typ_.

*स्वेदघ्न / स्वेद-स्तम्भन (svedaghna / sveda-stambhana)* — antihidrotic; sweat-checking (distinct from _svedana_, the sweating procedure). The reasoned Ayurvedic reading of sage's signature antihidrotic action, converging with 中醫 止汗 and Tibb _ḥābis li-l-ʿaraq_; cf. the floating-wheat (浮小麥) parallel. See _sage.typ_, _wheat.typ_.

*स्तन्य-शोषण / स्तन्य-नाशन (stanya-śoṣaṇa / stanya-nāśana)* — galactofuge; milk-drying (the *opposite* of _stanya-janana_, galactagogue). The traditional weaning use of sage. See _sage.typ_.

*हृद्य (hṛdya)* — cardiotonic / good-for-the-heart.

*चक्षुष्य (cakṣuṣya)* — eye-supportive.

*केश्य (keśya)* — hair-supportive.

*वर्ण्य (varṇya)* — complexion-improving.

*त्वच्य (tvacya)* — skin-supportive.

*स्तन्य-जनन / स्तन्य-वर्धन (stanya-janana / stanya-vardhana)* — galactagogue.

*शुक्रल (śukrala)* — sperm-promoting.

*वृष्य (vṛṣya)* — aphrodisiac.

*मूत्रल (mūtrala)* — diuretic.

*मूत्रकृच्छ्र-हर (mūtra-kṛcchra-hara)* — relieves dysuria.

*अश्मरि-भेदन (aśmari-bhedana)* — lithotriptic; the canonical prabhāva of gokṣura.

*कास-हर / श्वास-हर (kāsa-hara / śvāsa-hara)* — relieves cough / relieves dyspnea.

*कुष्ठ-घ्न (kuṣṭha-ghna)* — anti-skin-disease.

*कृमि-घ्न (kṛmi-ghna)* — anthelminthic.

*विष-घ्न (viṣa-ghna)* — anti-poison / detoxifying.

*व्रण-रोपण / व्रण-शोधन (vraṇa-ropaṇa / vraṇa-śodhana)* — wound-healing / wound-cleansing.

*दाह-प्रशमन (dāha-praśamana)* — relieves burning sensations.

*शूल-प्रशमन (śūla-praśamana)* — relieves colicky, griping, spasmodic pain (_śūla_ being the classical term for such pain). The signature reasoned action of chamomile and the register of the infant-colic and menstrual-cramp uses; converges with the Tibb _musakkin li-l-alam_ and the 中醫 理氣止痛. See _chamomile.typ_.

*शिरःशूल-हर (śiraḥ-śūla-hara)* — relieves headache; in a Pitta/heat-pattern reading, the register of cooling head-and-eye drugs. The reasoned rendering of the 中醫 平肝 headache indication. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*शोथहर (śothahara)* — reduces _śotha_ (edema, swelling); the anti-edema action, converging with 中醫 消腫 and Tibb use in _istisqā'_ (dropsy). See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*आर्तव-जनन / रजः-प्रवर्तन (ārtava-janana / rajaḥ-pravartana)* — emmenagogue; promotes menstrual flow. The basis of the pregnancy caution attached to several herbs in this book, and the direct counterpart of the Tibb *mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ*. Note that the genus name _Matricaria_ (< _mātrīx_, "womb") encodes this very action for chamomile. See _chamomile.typ_, _sage.typ_.

*तृष्णा-निग्रहण (tṛṣṇā-nigrahaṇa)* — quenches thirst.

*निद्रा-जनन (nidrā-janana)* — sleep-promoting.

*निद्रा-नाशन (nidrā-nāśana)* — sleep-destroying.

*मनः-प्रसादन (manas-prasādana)* — mind-calming / mood-elevating. The canonical prabhāva of jasmine.

*उच्छ्वास-शुद्धि (ucchvāsa-śuddhi)* — breath-clearing / breath-freshening (the after-meal mukhavāsa register).

*योगवाही (yogavāhī)* — carrier; the property of a substance that carries co-administered drugs to their target without losing its own essential qualities. The canonical prabhāva of ghee and honey.

=== Drug-form vocabulary

*चूर्ण (cūrṇa)* — powder; the basic compound-formula form.

*क्वाथ / काथ (kvātha / kāth)* — decoction.

*कषाय (kaṣāya)* — astringent decoction (note: same word as the astringent rasa — different but related senses).

*पेय / पेया (peya / peyā)* — thin gruel / rice-water; the lightest convalescent food.

*विलेपी (vilepī)* — thicker rice porridge.

*मण्ड (maṇḍa)* — rice-water alone; the lightest of the rice convalescent preparations.

*आहार (āhāra)* — food / diet (the broader dietetic category).

*यूष / यूष्य (yūṣa / yūṣya)* — soup, especially mung-soup or pulse-soup.

*मांस-रस (māṃsa-rasa)* — meat or fish broth; the classical convalescent preparation par excellence, given in Vāta-depletion and post-illness recovery. The direct counterpart of the Tibb *mā' al-samak* (fish broth) and of Chinese convalescent fish soup — a three-way convergence on broth as the food of recovery. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*स्वरस (svarasa)* — fresh juice.

*कल्क (kalka)* — wet paste (drugs ground with water to a paste).

*अर्क (arka)* — distillate (steam-distilled extract).

*आसव (āsava)* — fermented water-based preparation.

*अरिष्ट (ariṣṭa)* — fermented decoction (with sugar or jaggery and a fermenting medium).

*अवलेह / लेह (avaleha / leha)* — electuary / lickable medicated paste; the class to which Chyavanprash belongs.

*गुटिका / वटी (guṭikā / vaṭī)* — pill; the foundational pill form.

*घृत (ghṛta)* — medicated ghee; the foundational fat-based preparation.

*तैल (taila)* — medicated oil.

*भस्म (bhasma)* — calx / ash (mineral-pharmaceutical preparation).

*मण्डूर (mandūra)* — iron-rust-calx preparation.

*गुग्गुलु (guggulu)* — Commiphora-resin-based pill formulation class.

*अनुरस (anurasa)* — secondary or trailing taste; the subordinate rasa of a substance, as against its dominant one. Used in this book where a drug's classical attribution gives a primary and a slight taste — e.g. ginseng's madhura with tikta _anurasa_, rendering the Chinese 甘微苦. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*अनुपान (anupāna)* — vehicle / adjuvant; the substance with which a medicine is taken (warm water, milk, honey, ghee, wine, etc.). Selection of anupāna affects the action of the principal drug.

*संस्कार (saṃskāra)* — processing / refinement; the procedural transformation that changes a substance's qualities. The Ayurvedic equivalent of the Chinese 炮製.

*भावना (bhāvanā)* — trituration; the procedural soaking of one drug in another's juice or decoction.

*मुखवास (mukhavāsa)* — mouth-freshener; the after-meal seed-and-spice digestive practice (canonical fennel-seed-and-mishri).

=== Diet, time, and place — the climate framework

*ऋतुचर्या (ṛtucaryā)* — seasonal regimen; the seasonal-dietetic-and-behavioral framework. Source: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 6, Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya Sūtrasthāna 3. The six seasons of the Indian year:
- *शिशिर (śiśira)* — late winter (Jan-Feb)
- *वसन्त (vasanta)* — spring (Mar-Apr)
- *ग्रीष्म (grīṣma)* — summer (May-Jun)
- *वर्षा (varṣā)* — monsoon (Jul-Aug)
- *शरद् (śarad)* — autumn (Sep-Oct)
- *हेमन्त (hemanta)* — early winter (Nov-Dec)

*देश (deśa)* — place / climate / region. Source: Caraka Vimānasthāna 3, Sūtrasthāna 25. Three deśas:
- *जाङ्गल (jāṅgala)* — dry-arid (Pitta-Vāta climate)
- *आनूप (ānūpa)* — wet-marshy (Kapha climate)
- *साधारण (sādhāraṇa)* — moderate / balanced

*काल (kāla)* — time / season. Used in saṃyoga-viruddha and kāla-viruddha categories.

=== Viruddha — the incompatibility framework

*विरुद्ध (viruddha)* — incompatibility. Source: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26.81–101. Eighteen named categories of viruddha:
- *देश-विरुद्ध (deśa-viruddha)* — wrong-place / wrong-climate combination.
- *काल-विरुद्ध (kāla-viruddha)* — wrong-season / wrong-time combination.
- *अग्नि-विरुद्ध (agni-viruddha)* — wrong for the digestive fire state.
- *मात्रा-विरुद्ध (mātrā-viruddha)* — wrong quantity.
- *सात्म्य-विरुद्ध (sātmya-viruddha)* — wrong for the constitution.
- *दोष-विरुद्ध (doṣa-viruddha)* — aggravates an already-imbalanced doṣa.
- *संस्कार-विरुद्ध (saṃskāra-viruddha)* — wrong preparation procedure (e.g. cooked honey).
- *वीर्य-विरुद्ध (vīrya-viruddha)* — opposing potencies combined (e.g. fish + milk).
- *कोष्ठ-विरुद्ध (koṣṭha-viruddha)* — wrong for the bowel-state.
- *अवस्था-विरुद्ध (avasthā-viruddha)* — wrong for the body-state (sleep, exertion).
- *क्रम-विरुद्ध (krama-viruddha)* — wrong order of intake.
- *परिहार-विरुद्ध (parihāra-viruddha)* — taking incompatible foods within an interval.
- *उपचार-विरुद्ध (upacāra-viruddha)* — wrong procedural setting.
- *पाक-विरुद्ध (pāka-viruddha)* — wrong cooking method.
- *संयोग-विरुद्ध (saṃyoga-viruddha)* — wrong combination of substances (the named pairings: fish+milk, equal-weight ghee+honey, cooked honey, etc.).
- *हृद्-विरुद्ध (hṛd-viruddha)* — averse to the heart / palate.
- *सम्पद्-विरुद्ध (sampad-viruddha)* — wrong quality of the substance.
- *विधि-विरुद्ध (vidhi-viruddha)* — wrong intake procedure.

=== Anatomical / physiological terms

*धातु / सप्त-धातु (dhātu / sapta-dhātu, "seven tissues")* — the seven body tissues: रस (rasa, plasma), रक्त (rakta, blood), मांस (māṃsa, muscle), मेद (meda, fat), अस्थि (asthi, bone), मज्जा (majjā, marrow / nerve), शुक्र (śukra, reproductive). Each dhātu refines the previous.

*मल (mala)* — waste-products: feces (purīṣa), urine (mūtra), sweat (sveda).

*स्रोतस् / स्रोतांसि (srotas / srotāṃsi, "channels")* — the body's transport channels; thirteen named srotāṃsi in Caraka. Foundational to pathology: _srotorodha_ (blocked channel), _srotośodhana_ (channel-cleansing).

*गर्भाशय (garbhāśaya)* — uterus.

*स्तन्य (stanya)* — breast milk.

*शुक्र (śukra)* — sperm / reproductive essence.

*आर्तव (ārtava)* — menstrual blood / female reproductive essence (parallel to śukra).

*यकृत (yakṛt)* — liver.

*प्लीहन् (plīhan)* — spleen.

*हृदय (hṛdaya)* — heart.

*मस्तिष्क (mastiṣka)* — brain.

*कोष्ठ (koṣṭha)* — bowel-state: kruura-koṣṭha (hard / constipated), mṛdu-koṣṭha (soft), madhya-koṣṭha (balanced).

=== Named-disease vocabulary

*ज्वर (jvara)* — fever (the broad classical category; many sub-types).

*अतीसार (atīsāra)* — diarrhea.

*ग्रहणी (grahaṇī)* — malabsorption / IBS-spectrum syndrome.

*अम्लपित्त (amlapitta)* — hyperacidity / heat reflux.

*रक्तपित्त (rakta-pitta)* — bleeding from heat / Pitta-blood disorder.

*प्रमेह (prameha)* — urinary disorders / diabetic-spectrum syndrome (with twenty sub-types in Caraka; modern equivalents include diabetes mellitus).

*क्षय / राजयक्ष्मा (kṣaya / rāja-yakṣmā)* — wasting / consumption (mapped historically to tuberculosis).

*कास (kāsa)* — cough.

*श्वास (śvāsa)* — dyspnea / asthma.

*वातव्याधि (vāta-vyādhi)* — Vāta disorders (degenerative, neurological, musculoskeletal; many sub-types).

*आमवात (āmavāta)* — the classical joint disease combining _āma_ (metabolic toxin) with Vāta, presenting with migratory painful swollen joints, stiffness, and digestive impairment; broadly mapped to rheumatoid-arthritis-spectrum disease. Distinguished from _sandhi-vāta_ (degenerative/osteoarthritic joint Vāta). The register into which damp-type 濕痺 joint indications are translated. See _ginger.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*कुष्ठ (kuṣṭha)* — chronic skin diseases broadly (eighteen named sub-types in Caraka, the kuṣṭha-ghna drugs being those that act on this broad class).

*अश्मरी (aśmarī)* — urinary stones.

*मूत्र-कृच्छ्र (mūtra-kṛcchra)* — dysuria / painful urination.

*मूत्राघात (mūtrāghāta)* — urinary obstruction.

*गुल्म (gulma)* — abdominal masses.

*उदर (udara)* — abdominal disorders broadly.

*अपस्मार (apasmāra)* — epilepsy / convulsive disorders.

*उन्माद (unmāda)* — mental disturbance / psychosis-spectrum.

*अनिद्रा / निद्रा-नाश (anidrā / nidrā-nāśa)* — insomnia.

*क्लैब्य (klaibya)* — impotence / sexual debility.

*बन्ध्यत्व (bandhyatva)* — infertility.

*पाण्डु (pāṇḍu)* — anemia.

*श्वित्र / कुष्ठ-श्वित्र (śvitra / kuṣṭha-śvitra)* — vitiligo.

*अर्श (arśa)* — hemorrhoids.

*यक्ष्मा (yakṣmā)* — wasting / tuberculosis-spectrum.

*अर्बुद (arbuda)* — tumor / growth.

*श्लीपद (ślīpada)* — elephantiasis / lymphatic-filariasis-spectrum.

*अष्मरी (aśmarī)* — calculi / stones.

=== Classical source-texts

*चरकसंहिता (Caraka Saṃhitā)* — Caraka's _Compendium_, the foundational Ayurvedic text. Eight sthānas (sections): Sūtra-, Nidāna-, Vimāna-, Śārīra-, Indriya-, Cikitsā-, Kalpa-, Siddhi-sthāna. Compiled in approximately the 1st–2nd c. CE, building on earlier traditions; redacted by Dṛḍhabala (5th c.). The principal source for the dravyaguna framework. Translation cited throughout: Sharma & Dash, Chaukhamba Sanskrit Series Office.

*सुश्रुतसंहिता (Suśruta Saṃhitā)* — Suśruta's _Compendium_; the foundational surgical and anatomical text. Six sthānas plus the Uttara-tantra. Approximately 6th c. BCE → 1st c. CE in present form.

*अष्टांगहृदय (Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya)* — Vāgbhaṭa's _Heart of the Eight Branches_, c. 7th c. CE. Among the most-used Ayurvedic compendia.

*अष्टांगसंग्रह (Aṣṭāṅga Saṅgraha)* — Vāgbhaṭa's longer compendium.

*भावप्रकाश-निघण्टु (Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu)* — Bhāvamiśra's _Lexicon_, 16th c.; the most-used Ayurvedic drug-lexicon, with substantive dravyaguna entries.

*धन्वन्तरि-निघण्टु (Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu)* — earlier nighaṇṭu, 10th–13th c.

*राज-निघण्टु (Rāja Nighaṇṭu)* — Narahari's nighaṇṭu, 14th c.

*शार्ङ्गधर-संहिता (Sharangadhara Saṃhitā)* — Sharangadhara's compendium, 13th c.; the foundational source for compound-formula preparation methods.

*भैषज्य-रत्नावली (Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī)* — Govinda Dāsa's classical compound-formula compendium, 18th c.

*द्रव्यगुण-विज्ञान (Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna)* — P. V. Sharma's modern standard dravyaguna reference, 20th c.

*आयुर्वेदीय-फार्माकोपिया (Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India)* — the official Government of India drug-monograph series; the modern regulatory reference.

=== Selected drug-names and substance-specific terms

*लशुन (laśuna)* — the canonical Sanskrit name for *garlic* (_Allium sativum_). See _garlic.typ_. The synonym set includes:
- *रसोन (rasona)* — "one-taste-less"; the etymological pun on garlic's classical *five-rasa attribution* (the famous Bhāvaprakāśa attribution that garlic possesses five of six rasas, lacking _amla_ / sour).
- *महौषध (mahauṣadha)* — "the great medicine"; the highest classical praise, applied also to dried ginger (śuṇṭhī).
- *उग्रगन्ध (ugragandha)* — "strong-smelling."
- *भूतघ्न (bhūtaghna)* — "ghost-destroying"; the classical-apotropaic register; parallel to the European garlic-against-vampires folk tradition.

*लशुनक्षीरपाक (laśuna-kṣīrapāka)* — garlic-cooked-in-milk; the canonical Caraka rasāyana preparation (Cikitsāsthāna 1.4); 1 part garlic juice to 8 parts milk, reduced; cold-season rasāyana for Vāta-vyādhi. See _garlic.typ_. Modern Hindi descendant: *लसुने का दूध (_lasooney ka doodh_)*.

*तण्डुलीयक (tāṇḍulīyaka)* — _Amaranthus spinosus_; the classical Sanskrit name for spiny amaranth, with Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu entry. See _amaranth.typ_.

*रामदाना (rāmdānā)* — "Rama's grain"; the *post-Columbian* Indian name for amaranth grain (_A. caudatus, A. cruentus, A. hypochondriacus_); a *vrata fasting-day* grain. *चौलाई (chaulāī)* is the leaf-vegetable form. See _amaranth.typ_; cf. *kuṭṭū* (buckwheat, the parallel vrata-food).

*समुद्र-मन्थन (samudra-manthana)* — the *churning of the ocean*; the Purāṇic narrative of the devas and asuras churning the cosmic ocean for the *अमृत (amṛta)*, the nectar of immortality (synonymous with _soma_); during the churning, the demon *राहु (Rahu)* drank from the amṛta and was decapitated by Viṣṇu / Mohinī, with the soma-drops falling to earth and growing into garlic. Foundational to the Ayurvedic understanding of garlic's dual sacred-medicinal-and-tamasic character. See _garlic.typ_.

*वातव्याधि-रसायन (vāta-vyādhi-rasāyana)* — rejuvenative for Vāta-vyādhi (degenerative-neurological-musculoskeletal Vāta disorders); the foundational classical indication of laśuna per Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1.4.

*पुदीना (pudīnā)* — Persian-derived modern Hindi / Sanskrit for *garden mint* (_Mentha spicata_, _M. piperita_, _M. arvensis_). Distinct from European pennyroyal (*poejo* / *fūdhanaj*); see _poejo.typ_ for the species-identification disambiguation.

*दमनक / नागदमनी (damanaka / nāgadamanī)* — aromatic Artemisia (commonly _A. pallens_, _A. nilagirica_, or _A. vulgaris_) of classical Sanskrit usage, valued for fragrance and the spring _damanakāropaṇa_ worship-rite as much as for medicine. *Not* European wormwood (_A. absinthium_), which is post-classical in India under the borrowed Unani name *अफ़संतीन (afsantīn)* / *विलायती अफ़संतीन (vilāyatī afsantīn)*, "foreign afsantīn." See _wormwood.typ_.

*लवङ्ग (lavaṅga)* — clove (_Syzygium aromaticum_); also *देवकुसुम (devakusuma, "god-flower")*; Hindi *लौंग (laung)*. Kaṭu-tikta in rasa but classically *śīta (cooling) in vīrya* — a cross-system divergence from the "hot" Chinese/Tibb/Galenic reading; a leading _chardighna_ (antiemetic) and _dīpana_ spice. See _cloves.typ_.

*अक्षोट / अक्षोड (akṣoṭa / akṣoḍa)* — walnut (_Juglans regia_); also *कर्कट (karkaṭa)*, Hindi *अखरोट (akhrōṭ)*; the sweet-unctuous-heating _medhya_ (nervine/brain-tonic), _balya_, and _vṛṣya_ nut. *Not* the American black walnut (_J. nigra_), which has no classical Sanskrit name and whose green-hull drug is astringent-bitter, not tonic. See _black-walnut.typ_.

*द्राक्षा / मृद्वीका (drākṣā / mṛdvīkā)* — grape / raisin (_Vitis vinifera_); also *गोस्तनी (gostanī, "cow-udder")*. The archetypal madhura–śīta–snigdha fruit, classically *फलोत्तमा (phalottamā, "best of fruits")*, a leading _vṛṣya-rasāyana_ and _raktapitta-hara_ dravya. The Persian-derived pharmacy names *मुनक्का (munakkā,* large seeded) and *किशमिश (kishmish,* small seedless) distinguish the dried forms; the fermented tonic *द्राक्षासव / द्राक्षारिष्ट (Drākṣāsava / Drākṣāriṣṭa)* is a classical _āsava/ariṣṭa_. See _raisins.typ_.

*सेब / सेव (seb / seva)* — apple (_Malus domestica_); a *Persian loan* (from سیب _sīb_), *post-classical* in India (not in the core saṃhitā fruit-lists; cultivated anciently only in the Himalayan/Kashmiri fringe). Reasoned dravyaguna reading: madhura-kaṣāya, śīta, Vāta-aggravating when raw hence best stewed-and-spiced. See _apple.typ_.

*मत्स्य (matsya)* — fish, the general classical category (Caraka Sūtrasthāna 27; Suśruta Sūtrasthāna 46): madhura rasa, guru-snigdha guṇa, uṣṇa vīrya, madhura vipāka; Vāta-pacifying, Kapha- and Pitta-aggravating; _balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_, _vṛṣya_. Sub-classified by habitat: *नादीमत्स्य (nādī-matsya)* riverine/freshwater — classically the *heavier* type; *समुद्रमत्स्य (samudra-matsya)* marine — the *lighter*. *A standing cross-system divergence:* classical Tibb reverses both judgements, reading fresh fish as *Cold-and-Moist* (not uṣṇa) and *river* fish as the lighter. The canonical *matsya-kṣīra viruddha* (fish with milk) is at Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_, _salmon.typ_, _sardines.typ_.

*मूंगफली (mūṅgphalī, "mung-fruit")* — *peanut* in Hindi; a *modern vernacular coinage by analogy* with the familiar mung pulse, *not* a Sanskrit or dravyaguna lemma — the peanut is post-Columbian in India and has no classical name. Noted because groundnut oil and peanut confections are now dietetically central in western and southern India, so a substance with no classical standing whatever became a staple within about two centuries. See _peanut.typ_.

*नीली / नीलिनी (nīlī / nīlinī)* — *indigo* as an Ayurvedic drug: *_Indigofera tinctoria_* L. (Fabaceae), from *नील (nīla,* "dark blue, black"). *A different plant from the Chinese indigo sources* — same pigment (indigotin), unrelated botany, independent tradition. Tikta-kaṣāya rasa, laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa guṇa, *uṣṇa* vīrya (flagged — if correct, a head-on divergence from the Chinese and Tibb *cold* readings of the same pigment), kaṭu vipāka; *kṛmighna*, *kuṣṭhaghna*, *viṣaghna*, *keśya*. The living preparation is *नीलीभृङ्गादि तैल (Nīlībhṛṅgādi taila)*, a keśya hair oil. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*गवेधुका / गवेधु (gavedhukā / gavedhu)* — a grass-grain of the Sanskrit lexicons, identified with *Coix* (Monier-Williams gives _Coix barbata_). *Register matters here:* the name occurs in Vedic/ritual and lexicographic contexts as a wild grain, *not* in a developed dravyaguna monograph — so Job's tears is *named but not pharmacologically elaborated* in Sanskrit, a middle case between classical attestation and total absence, and tiered accordingly. See _coix.typ_.

*छत्राक / कवक (chatraka / kavaka)* — mushrooms/fungi in the saṃhitā dietetics, where they are classed as *heavy and generally unsuitable* food. Noted because the unfavourable classical treatment of fungi means Ayurveda offers *no congenial category* to receive a medicinal fungus such as Poria. See _fuling.typ_.

*नाशपाती (nāshpātī)* — pear (_Pyrus_ spp.); a *Persian loan*, *post-classical* in India. Reasoned reading: madhura-kaṣāya, guru, śīta, Pitta-pacifying and demulcent-yet-astringent, best cooked for cold/Vāta constitutions. See _pear.typ_.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the substantive treatment of the framework. The glossary here gives the working vocabulary._

=== Fundamental concepts

*طب (ṭibb)* — medicine; the term denoting both classical Greco-Arabic medicine and the broader "Prophetic medicine" tradition (_Ṭibb al-Nabawī_).

*طب اليوناني / يوناني (Ṭibb al-Yūnānī / Yūnānī)* — "Greek medicine"; the South-Asian (principally Indian-Pakistani) designation of Greco-Arabic Tibb. Regulated under AYUSH in India; BUMS (Bachelor of Unani Medicine and Surgery) is the clinical degree.

*طب نبوي (Ṭibb al-Nabawī)* — Prophetic medicine; the distinct-but-allied tradition based on Qur'ān and hadith. Canonical compendium: Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_.

*طب سنتی (Ṭibb-e Sonnati)* — Iranian classical medicine; the Persian-tradition designation; modernly revived as integrated traditional medicine under Iranian Ministry of Health.

*أخلاط (akhlāṭ)* — humors; the four classical humors:
- *دم (dam)* — blood; Hot-and-Moist; sanguine temperament; produced in the liver.
- *بلغم (balgham)* — phlegm; Cold-and-Moist; phlegmatic temperament; produced in the digestion.
- *صفراء (ṣafrā')* — yellow bile; Hot-and-Dry; bilious / choleric temperament; in gallbladder.
- *سوداء (sawdā')* — black bile; Cold-and-Dry; melancholic temperament; in spleen.

*مزاج (mizāj, "temperament")* — temperament. Two senses: (1) the individual constitutional type (with 9 categories: balanced + 8 imbalances); (2) the drug's humoral profile (Hot/Cold + Dry/Moist + degree).

*مزاج معتدل (mizāj muʿtadil)* — balanced temperament (ideal individual mizāj).

*درجات (darajāt, "degrees")* — the 1st through 4th degree grading of drug-mizāj intensity. 1st degree: mild. 2nd: moderate. 3rd: strong. 4th: extreme / dangerous (requires strict dose control — e.g. opium). Source: Avicenna, _Qānūn_ II.

*أرواح / أرواح الثلاث (arwāḥ / al-arwāḥ al-thalāth, "the three spirits")*:
- *روح طبيعي (rūḥ ṭabīʿī)* — natural spirit; in the liver; governs vegetative function.
- *روح حيواني (rūḥ ḥaywānī)* — animal / vital spirit; in the heart; governs circulation.
- *روح نفساني (rūḥ nafsānī)* — psychic / nervous spirit; in the brain; governs sensation and cognition.

*أسباب ستة (asbāb sittah, "the six necessary causes")*:
1. *هواء (hawā')* — air / climate / environment.
2. *مأكول ومشروب (maʾkūl wa-mashrūb)* — food and drink.
3. *حركة وسكون جسماني (ḥarakah wa-sukūn jismānī)* — bodily movement and rest.
4. *حركة وسكون نفساني (ḥarakah wa-sukūn nafsānī)* — emotional movement and rest.
5. *نوم ويقظة (nawm wa-yaqẓah)* — sleep and wakefulness.
6. *احتباس واستفراغ (iḥtibās wa-istifrāgh)* — retention and excretion.

=== Action vocabulary (Af'āl)

*أفعال (af'āl, sing. fi'l, "actions")* — the canonical Tibb action vocabulary:

*مفتح (mufattiḥ)* — deobstruent; opens blocked channels (liver, spleen, kidney, vessels).

*محلل (muḥallil)* — resolvent; dissolves accumulations (cysts, swellings).

*مقوي (muqawwī)* — tonic / strengthener. Specified by organ:
- *مقوي القلب (muqawwī al-qalb)* — cardiotonic
- *مقوي الدماغ (muqawwī al-dimāgh)* — brain tonic
- *مقوي المعدة (muqawwī al-miʿda)* — gastric tonic
- *مقوي الكبد (muqawwī al-kabid)* — hepatic tonic
- *مقوي الكلى (muqawwī al-kalā)* — renal tonic
- *مقوي الباه (muqawwī al-bāh)* — aphrodisiac
- *مقوي الروح (muqawwī al-rūḥ)* — vital-spirit tonic
- *مقوي الأعصاب (muqawwī al-aʿṣāb)* — nerve tonic

*مفرح (mufariḥ)* — cardiac exhilarant / mood-elevator. The canonical mood-elevating action; the Mufarriḥ class of formulas (Mufarriḥ-i Shāhī, Mufarriḥ-i Muʿtadil) are foundational Unani cardiacs.

*مرطب (muraṭṭib)* — humectant; moistens dryness.

*مبرد (mubarriḍ)* — cooling; refrigerant.

*منعش (munaʿʿish)* — refreshing / reviving; the invigorating-refreshing action (e.g. the reasoned Tibb reading of cooling teas). See _white-tea.typ_.

*ملين (mulayyin)* — gentle laxative.

*مسهل (mushil)* — purgative; stronger than mulayyin.

*قابض (qābiḍ)* — astringent; binds (in cases of diarrhea, bleeding).

*مدر (mudirr)* — diuretic / discharge-promoting. _Mudirr li-l-bawl_ (diuretic), _mudirr li-l-laban_ (galactagogue), _mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ_ (emmenagogue).

*منوم (munawwim)* — hypnotic / sleep-inducing.

*مخدر (mukhaddir)* — narcotic / numbing.

*معرق (muʿarriq)* — diaphoretic.

*حابس للعرق (ḥābis li-l-ʿaraq)* — sweat-arresting; antihidrotic (the *opposite* of _muʿarriq_). The Tibb reading of sage's signature action, converging with 中醫 止汗 and the reasoned Ayurvedic _svedaghna_. See _sage.typ_.

*منفث / مخرج البلغم (munaffith / mukhrij al-balgham)* — expectorant.

*مخرج القيح (mukhrij al-qayḥ)* — pus-expelling; the humoral counterpart of the 中醫 排膿 and of the reasoned Ayurvedic _vraṇa-śodhana_. See _coix.typ_.

*مسكّن / مسكّن للألم (musakkin / musakkin li-l-alam)* — calming, pain-allaying; analgesic, especially of colicky and cramping pain. The Tibb counterpart of _śūla-praśamana_ and of 理氣止痛. See _chamomile.typ_.

*مجفف (mujaffif)* — desiccant; drying of excess moisture in tissues, wounds, and mucosa. Distinguished from *qābiḍ* (astringent/binding) and from *mudirr* (discharge-promoting). See _chamomile.typ_, _fuling.typ_, _sage.typ_.

*مقيء (muqīʾ)* — emetic.

*محرك الباه (muḥarrik al-bāh)* — aphrodisiac.

*محرك الأخلاط (muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ)* — humor-stirring; the cautionary class — can release latent disease in the susceptible.

*محسن (muḥsin)* — improver. _Muḥsin al-bashara_ (skin-improving), _muḥsin al-hawḍm_ (digestion-improving), _muḥsin al-lawn_ (complexion-improving).

*جالي (jālī)* — cleansing.

*كاسر الرياح (kāsir li-l-riyāḥ)* — carminative ("wind-breaker").

*مفطت (mufattit)* — breaker / disintegrator. _Mufattit li-l-ḥaṣā_ (stone-breaking; cf. aśmari-bhedana).

*محسن النفس (muḥsin al-nafas)* — breath-improver.

*مغذي (mughadhdhī)* — nourishing.

*هاضم (hāḍim)* — digestive.

*حافظ (ḥāfiẓ)* — preservative / protective. _Ḥāfiẓ li-l-jasad_ (body-preserving).

=== Drug-form vocabulary

*مفردات (mufradāt, sing. mufrad)* — simples; single drugs.

*مركبات (murakkabāt, sing. murakkab)* — compounds; multi-drug formulas.

*مصلح (muṣliḥ)* — corrective / adjuvant; the substance traditionally paired to correct a drug's adverse property without diminishing its action. Classical pairings textually fixed.

*بدل (badal)* — substitute; the classical alternative drug used where the principal is unavailable. Listed in formal _badal_ tables.

*سفوف (sufūf)* — powder.

*معجون (maʿjūn)* — electuary; herb powders kneaded into honey or syrup base. The Unani equivalent of the Ayurvedic _avaleha_. Examples: Maʿjūn-i Falāsifah (Philosophers' Electuary), Maʿjūn-i Najāḥ (for asthma), Maʿjūn Chyavan-prāsh (the Unani adoption of Cyavanaprāśa).

*شربت (sharbat)* — syrup; sweet liquid preparation. Examples: Sharbat-i Banafsha (violet syrup), Sharbat-i Faulād (iron syrup), Sharbat-i Bazūrī (seed-syrup, diuretic).

*خميرة (khamīrah)* — fermented preparation; jam-like; the Mufarriḥ class. Examples: Khamīrah-i Marwārīd (pearl-electuary), Khamīrah-i Gawzaban (borage-based cardiac).

*جوارش (jawārish)* — a class of *digestive electuaries* (semi-solid stomachic pastes), typically warming-carminative, taken to strengthen digestion; the Unani register in which aromatic spices such as clove (_qaranful_) are canonical. See _cloves.typ_.

*جوشاندة (joshānda)* — decoction.

*مغلي (maghlī)* — a simple hot infusion or boiled preparation; the everyday household form of mild aromatic simples across the Arab world (the form in which *bābūnaj* is actually taken). See _chamomile.typ_.

*روغن (roghan)* — the Unani/Persian class of *medicated oils* (Persian _roghan_, "oil"): e.g. *roghan-i bābūnah* (chamomile oil), the direct continuation of the Galenic infused-oil form _oleum chamomillae_. The Tibb counterpart of the Ayurvedic *taila*. See _chamomile.typ_.

*ماء السمك (māʾ al-samak)* — fish broth; the classical Tibb convalescent preparation for debility, counterpart of the Ayurvedic *māṃsa-rasa*. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*عرق (ʿarq)* — distillate / hydrosol. Examples: ʿArq-i Yāsamīn (jasmine hydrosol), ʿarq sūs (Egyptian street licorice-decoction).

*مرهم (marham)* — ointment.

*طلاء (ṭilāʾ)* — liniment.

*اطريفل (Itrīfal)* — triphalā-based preparation class (direct phonetic adoption of Sanskrit _tri-phalā_). Examples: Itrīfal Ṣaghīr (small / equivalent to Triphalā cūrṇa), Itrīfal Kabīr, Itrīfal Aftīmūn (with Cuscuta).

*حب (ḥabb, pl. ḥubūb)* — pill.

*سكنجبين (sakanjabīn)* — Persian-Arabic oxymel (honey + vinegar, often with mint); descendant of Hippocratic _oxymeli_; foundational Unani household medicine.

=== Anatomical / physiological terms

*صدر (ṣadr)* — chest.

*رئة (riʾa)* — lung.

*كبد (kabid)* — liver.

*طحال (ṭiḥāl)* — spleen.

*كلى (kulā)* — kidneys.

*معدة (miʿda)* — stomach.

*أمعاء (amʿāʾ)* — intestines.

*دماغ (dimāgh)* — brain.

*قلب (qalb)* — heart.

*أعصاب (aʿṣāb)* — nerves.

*مثانة (mathāna)* — bladder.

*رحم (raḥim)* — uterus.

*عروق (ʿurūq)* — vessels.

=== Named-disease vocabulary

*أمراض (amrāḍ)* — diseases.

*أمراض البلغم (amrāḍ al-balgham)* — phlegmatic diseases.

*أمراض السوداء (amrāḍ al-sawdā')* — melancholic / black-bile diseases.

*أمراض الصفراء (amrāḍ al-ṣafrā')* — bilious / yellow-bile diseases.

*أمراض المفاصل (amrāḍ al-mafāṣil)* — joint diseases.

*أمراض الأعصاب (amrāḍ al-aʿṣāb)* — nervous diseases.

*أمراض الجلد (amrāḍ al-juld)* — skin diseases.

*استسقاء (istisqā')* — dropsy / edema.

*يرقان (yaraqān)* — jaundice.

*سعال (suʿāl)* — cough. _Suʿāl muzmin_ — chronic cough.

*ربو (rabw)* — asthma.

*احتقان (iḥtiqān)* — congestion.

*سدد (suddad)* — obstructions / blockages.

*ضعف (ḍuʿf)* — debility / weakness.

*خفقان (khafqān)* — palpitations.

*أرق (araq)* — insomnia.

*صرع (ṣarʿ)* — epilepsy.

*مالنخوليا (mālinkhūliyā)* — melancholia (the borrowed Greek term).

*حصى الكلى / حصى المثانة (ḥaṣā al-kalā / ḥaṣā al-mathāna)* — renal / bladder stones.

*عسر البول (ʿusr al-bawl)* — dysuria.

*الباه (al-bāh)* — sexual function.

*عقم (ʿuqm)* — infertility.

=== Tibb al-Nabawī-attested substances and concepts

*حبة السوداء (ḥabba al-sawdāʾ, "the black seed")* — _Nigella sativa_, Prophetic-medicine substance. Famous hadith: "In the black seed there is healing for every disease except death" (Bukhari, Muslim).

*تلبينة (talbīna)* — thin barley-flour broth; Prophetic-medicine convalescent food. Hadith: "Talbīna is comforting for the heart of the patient and dispels some of the sadness" (Bukhari).

*كمأة (kamʾa)* — truffle; Prophetic medicine for eyes.

*خل (khall)* — vinegar; Prophetic-medicine condiment ("How excellent the condiment vinegar is" — Muslim).

*حجامة (ḥijāma)* — cupping; Prophetic-medicine procedure.

*سنا (sanā)* — senna; Prophetic-medicine laxative.

*قسط / كست (qusṭ / kust)* — costus, _Saussurea costus_; Prophetic-medicine substance.

*عجوة (ʿajwa)* — the Madinan date variety with the canonical "seven dates / day → no poison or magic" hadith (Bukhari).

*سدرة المنتهى (Sidrat al-Muntahā)* — the Lote-Tree-of-the-Furthest-Limit; the cosmic sidr (Ziziphus) tree of Qur'ān 53:14–16.

=== Classical source-texts

*القانون في الطب (al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb)* — Avicenna's _Canon of Medicine_, c. 1025; the foundational medieval Arabic medical work, 5 books, Book II being the simple-drug pharmacology. The principal source for Tibb mizāj attributions throughout the materia medica.

*الجامع لمفردات الأدوية والأغذية (Kitāb al-Jāmiʿ li-Mufradāt al-Adwiya wa-l-Aghdhiya)* — Ibn al-Bayṭār's great pharmacopoeia, 13th c.; ~1400 simples, comprehensive Andalusian-Maghrebi reference.

*الحاوي (al-Ḥāwī)* — al-Rāzī's _Comprehensive Book_, c. 925.

*التصريف (al-Taṣrīf)* — al-Zahrāwī's _Taṣrīf_, c. 1000.

*تذكرة أولي الألباب (Tadhkirat ūlī al-Albāb)* — al-Anṭākī's late-medieval compendium, late 16th c.

*طب أكبري (Ṭibb-e Akbarī)* — Hakim Arzānī's Mughal-period Unani text.

*بياض كبير (Bayāz-e Kabīr)* — Hakim Kabīruddīn's modern Unani prescription manual.

*خزائن الأدوية (Khazāyin al-Adwiya)* — Hakim Najmul Ghani Khan's massive modern Urdu Unani pharmacopoeia, ~5 vols.

*مخزن الأدوية (Makhzan al-Adwiya)* — ʿAqīlī Khurāsānī's Persian compendium, late 18th c.

*الطب النبوي (al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī)* — Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya's compilation of Prophetic-medicine substances and recommendations, 14th c.

=== Selected drug-names, formulas, and substance-specific terms

*ثوم (thūm)* — the canonical Arabic name for *garlic* (_Allium sativum_); same in Persian; in Urdu both *thūm* (formal Unani) and *lahsun* (Hindi-derived). See _garlic.typ_.
- *فوم (fūm)* — Qur'ānic name for garlic in Qur'ān 2:61 (Sūrat al-Baqarah), the only Qur'ānic garlic verse (in the wilderness-Israelites-demand-Egyptian-foods passage); the standard exegetical identification is garlic.
- *ثوم بستاني (thūm bustānī)* — garden / cultivated garlic.
- *ثوم بري (thūm barrī)* — wild garlic.

*ترياق (tiryāq, "theriac")* — the foundational Greco-Arabic-medieval-European *universal antidote*; complex compound preparation against poison, plague, and humoral disorder. The most famous: *Theriac Andromachi* (~60 ingredients including viper-flesh; imperial-Roman preparation) and *Mithridatium* (the legendary universal-antidote of Mithridates VI of Pontus). Extraordinarily expensive; reserved for the wealthy.

*ترياق الفقراء (Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ, "the poor man's theriac")* — the canonical Tibb appellation for *garlic* — held to provide the same antidote-and-prophylactic protection at vanishing cost. Direct Arabic-Tibb successor to Galen's Greek "country-folk's theriac" reading. See _garlic.typ_.

*فوذنج (fūdhanaj, also fawtanaj, fūthanaj)* — the canonical Tibb name for *pennyroyal* (_Mentha pulegium_); Persian *pūdane-ye kūhī* ("mountain pūdane"); see _poejo.typ_.

*نعنع (naʿnaʿ)* — Arabic generic for *mint* (typically garden mint, _M. spicata_ or _M. piperita_); distinct from فوذنج (fūdhanaj, pennyroyal); the canonical Egyptian / Levantine mint-tea register.

*سدهاب (sadhāb)* — *rue* (_Ruta graveolens_); the Mediterranean-Arab classical emmenagogue and abortifacient, with caveats paralleling pennyroyal.

*بابونج (bābūnaj)* — *chamomile* (_Matricaria chamomilla_, _Chamaemelum nobile_); the canonical Tibb digestive-and-calming bitter Asteraceae; the Tibb framework for digestive-bitter Asteraceae generally, applied by extension to other Asteraceae digestives like the South-American _Pluchea sagittalis_ (see _lucera.typ_). Also *بابونة (bābūna)*, Persian *bābūne*, Urdu *bābūnah* / *gul-e bābūnah*; ultimate etymology *unresolved* (a Persian-mediated route is proposed; a direct derivation from Greek _chamaímēlon_ should not be asserted). *Mizāj:* Hot-and-Dry, 1st degree in the standard placement (2nd in some sources) — *note the divergence from the modern popular reading of chamomile as "cooling,"* which this book flags rather than reconciles. *No Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation.* See _chamomile.typ_.

*أقحوان (uqḥuwān)* — *a naming trap, recorded as such.* Modern Arabic uses this word for *chrysanthemum*, but in the medieval pharmacological corpus _uqḥuwān_ denotes a *chamomile- or feverfew-class* daisy (_Matricaria_ / _Anthemis_ / _Tanacetum parthenium_ group), *not* the East Asian _Chrysanthemum_ cultigen. Citing Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār _sub voce uqḥuwān_ as Tibb attestation for 菊花 would therefore be a misattribution produced by modern nomenclature. Identification flagged for verification against a specific _al-Jāmiʿ_ edition. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*دمعة أيوب (damʿat Ayyūb, "Job's tear")* — *a second naming trap.* The modern scientific-Arabic name for *Job's tears* (_Coix lacryma-jobi_) is a *calque of the European botanical epithet*, coined in modern nomenclature. Because *Ayyūb* (Job) is a Qur'ānic prophet (Qur'ān 21:83–84; 38:41–44), the name can be misread as Prophetic-medical pedigree: *there is none* — the plant is absent from the classical Arabic corpus entirely, and the scriptural resonance is an artefact of translating a 17th–18th-c. European metaphor. See _coix.typ_.

*بلطي (bulṭī) / مشط (mushṭ, "comb")* — tilapia: *bulṭī* the standard modern Egyptian name (today Egypt's most-eaten fish), *mushṭ* the Levantine name (for the comb-like dorsal fin) applied to Sea-of-Galilee tilapia in the "St. Peter's fish" trade register. No species-specific classical monograph has been located; the fish falls under generic *samak al-nahr* (river fish), which in the Avicennan reading is *Cold-and-Moist* and the *lighter*, more digestible class — the inverse of the Ayurvedic reading of riverine fish as heavier. See _tilapia.typ_, _fish.typ_.

*أفسنتين (afsantīn, also afsantīn rūmī)* — *wormwood* (_Artemisia absinthium_); a direct loan from Greek _apsínthion_, and one of the tradition's principal *muqawwī al-maʿida* (stomach-tonic) and hepatic-deobstruent bitters, Hot in the 1st and Dry in the 2nd–3rd degree per Avicenna. Distinct from the native desert Artemisia *شيح (shīḥ)* (_A. herba-alba_, _A. judaica_) and from *درمنة / شيح خراساني (dirmana / shīḥ khurāsānī)*, the Khorasani wormseed (_A. cina_) that yields santonin. A Greco-Arabic (Dioscoridean) drug, *not* a Ṭibb al-Nabawī substance. See _wormwood.typ_.

*قرنفل (qaranful)* — clove (_Syzygium aromaticum_); Persian *میخک (mīkhak, "little nail")* — the same nail-metaphor as Chinese 丁 and Latin _clāvus_. Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree (Avicenna); a leading *muqawwī* (cardiac/brain/stomach tonic), *mufarriḥ*, *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac), and dental analgesic (the eugenol toothache use); canonical in *jawārish* and *maʿjūn* preparations and in *ʿiṭr* perfumery. See _cloves.typ_.

*جوز (jawz, also jawz shāmī "Levantine walnut")* — walnut (_Juglans regia_); Persian *گردو (gerdū)*. The nut is a Hot brain-and-body tonic and *muqawwī al-bāh*; the green hull *قشر الجوز (qishr al-jawz)* is astringent (*qābiḍ*), anthelmintic (*dāfiʿ li-l-dīdān*), and the classical black hair-*dye* (*ṣābigh*) — the Old-World analogue applied by extension to the American black walnut (_J. nigra_), which is *not* classical in Tibb. See _black-walnut.typ_.

*زبيب / عنب (zabīb / ʿinab)* — raisin / grape (_Vitis vinifera_); the deseeded raisin is *منقّى (munaqqā)*; Persian *انگور (angūr)*, *کشمش (kishmish)*, *مویز (mavīz)*. Hot-Moist (1st degree); a leading *muqawwī al-kabid* (liver tonic), nutritive (*mughadhdhī*), and gentle laxative (*mulayyin*). Strong Ṭibb al-Nabawī register: the Qurʾānic *أعناب (aʿnāb)* and the Prophetic *نبيذ الزبيب (nabīdh al-zabīb)*, non-intoxicating raisin-water. See _raisins.typ_.

*لوف (lūf)* — *Arum* species in the Arabic materia medica, inherited from the Greek *ἄρον (áron)* of Dioscorides: an acrid aroid tuber, hot and sharp, requiring *cooking or drying* before use, applied to swellings and skin complaints. Attested in Ibn al-Bayṭār's _al-Jāmiʿ_. Noted as the Greco-Arabic member of the acrid-Araceae family — *a structural parallel to 半夏, not an attestation of it*. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*طحينة (ṭaḥīna) / tahini* — Levantine sesame paste: *hulled* seed, lightly roasted or raw, ground fine and loose — pale, pourable, mild. The base of *hummus*, *baba ghanoush*, *tarator* and *ḥalāwa*. From the root *ط-ح-ن (ṭ-ḥ-n)*, "to grind" (*طحين, ṭaḥīn*, "flour"), so the name means simply "the ground thing." *Because the hull is removed, tahini is much lower in both calcium and oxalate than whole-seed sesame* — the ~1000 mg/100 g calcium figure quoted for sesame does not apply to it. Distinct from Chinese *芝麻醬* and Japanese *練り胡麻*. See _sesame-seeds.typ_.

*فول سوداني (fūl sūdānī, "Sudanese bean")* — *peanut* in Arabic; Egyptian colloquial *سوداني (sūdānī)*. *The name is evidence of the transmission route*: the Old World received the peanut *from Africa*, not from the Americas, and the Arabic name records the second leg rather than the origin. *No classical Tibb attestation* — a post-Columbian plant absent from Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār; Persian *بادام زمینی (bādām-e zamīnī,* "earth almond") assimilates it to the nut class. See _peanut.typ_.

*نيل / نيلج (nīl / nīlaj)* — *indigo*; attested in the Arabic materia medica (Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_), and *the word is a direct borrowing of Sanskrit नील (nīla)* — the clearest evidence of the transmission route, the dye and its name arriving together from India. Mizāj *Cold and Dry* (degree flagged for verification), with a *predominantly topical* register: *qābiḍ*, *mujaffif*, *jālī*, *muḥallil* for hot swellings, ulcers and skin complaints, plus use as a hair and beard dye (*ṣābigh*). *The Arabic drug is Indian indigo (Indigofera), not the Chinese lime-fermented 青黛.* *No Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation* — the Prophetic hair-dye register concerns *ḥinnāʾ* and *katam*, not indigo. The word also travelled onward: Arabic *nīl* → Spanish/Portuguese *añil / anil* → the chemical term *aniline*. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*جنسنج / جينسنغ (jinsinj / jīnsinġ)* — *ginseng*; a *modern transliteration*, not a classical drug-name. _Panax ginseng_ is *absent from Avicenna's Qānūn and from Ibn al-Bayṭār's al-Jāmiʿ* — a high-value, slow-growing root consumed close to its source, which never entered the westward trade that carried musk, camphor, cinnamon and rhubarb. Modern Unani has adopted it as a post-classical *muqawwī*; the plausible class-level *badal* within the actual Tibb pharmacopoeia would be the native restorative-aphrodisiac roots *بهمن سرخ / بهمن سفید (bahman surkh / bahman safed)*, *شقاقل (shaqāqul,* _Pastinaca_), and *تودری (tūdarī)*. *No Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation, and none possible.* See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*عوسج (ʿawsaj)* — the Arabian desert boxthorn (_Lycium shawii_ / _L. depressum_), a native thorny-shrub congener of the genus _Lycium_, attested in Arabic lexicography and a few hadith as a thorn-plant. A *different species* from the Chinese goji (_L. barbarum_), which has no classical Tibb entry; noted to prevent lending the modern goji a Nabawī pedigree via the congener. See _goji-berry.typ_.

*مريمية / قصعين (maryamiyya / qaṣʿīn)* — *sage* (_Salvia officinalis_, and the sibling Levantine _S. fruticosa_/_S. triloba_ often sold under the same name); also *سالمية (sālmiyya)*, Persian *مریم‌گلی (maryam-golī)*. Hot-Dry (1st–2nd degree); a *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain/nerve tonic), *qābiḍ* and *ḥābis li-l-ʿaraq* (astringent, sweat-arrester), and digestive-throat aromatic; the living Levantine preparation is *شاي مريمية (shāy maryamiyya)*, sage tea. The name *maryamiyya* ("Mary's herb") is a Levantine *Christian* Marian folk association, *not* a Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation. See _sage.typ_.

*تفّاح (tuffāḥ)* — apple (_Malus domestica_); a major classical Tibb fruit, above all a *cardiac tonic and exhilarant* (*muqawwī al-qalb*, *mufarriḥ*) — the apple and its very scent (*shamm al-tuffāḥ*) gladden the heart; the basis of the *Tuffāḥī* cordials (*Sharbat-e Sīb*, *Murabbā-e Sīb*). Mizāj variety-graded (sweet apple temperate–warm-moist; sour apple cold-dry). Persian *سیب (sīb)*. See _apple.typ_.

*كمّثرى (kummathrā)* — pear (_Pyrus_ spp.); a classical Tibb fruit, Cold-and-Dry, *qābiḍ* (astringent) and *muqawwī al-maʿida* (stomachic), cooling and thirst-quenching, best eaten ripe or cooked. Persian *گلابی (golābī)* / *امرود (amrūd)*; *nomenclature caution:* _amrūd_ historically meant pear but in *modern Urdu denotes guava* — unambiguous Urdu pear = *ناشپاتی (nāshpātī)*. See _pear.typ_.

*سيكنجبين الثوم (Sikanjabīn-i Thūm)* — *garlic oxymel*; garlic + vinegar + honey syrup; the *Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ* in syrup form.

*طوم (toum) / آيولي (ālyūlī)* — Levantine garlic-and-oil-emulsion sauce (Toum is Lebanese; aïoli the Provençal cognate; *alioli* the Catalan; *skordalia* the Greek). Direct culinary-medical descendant of the classical Greco-Roman garlic-paste tradition observing the muṣliḥ rule (vinegar / lemon as adjuvant). See _garlic.typ_.

*مورتم (moretum)* — Latin garlic-and-herb-and-cheese paste of Roman peasant cuisine; the canonical preparation described in the *Moretum* attributed to Virgil. The Mediterranean garlic-paste tradition's foundational form.

== Greco-Roman & Hippocratic-Galenic Tradition

=== The humoral framework

*The four humors* (Greek _τέσσαρες χυμοί_, _téssares chumoí_; Latin _humores_):
- *αἷμα / sanguis* — blood
- *φλέγμα / pituita* — phlegm
- *χολή / bilis flava* — yellow bile / choler
- *μέλαινα χολή / atra bilis / melancholia* — black bile / melancholy

The Latin / Greek-Latin Greek transmission gave English "sanguine," "phlegmatic," "choleric," "melancholic" — the four classical temperaments. This is also the direct ancestor of the Arabic akhlāṭ system.

*The four qualities*: _hot / cold_ (calidus/frigidus, θερμός/ψυχρός) and _moist / dry_ (humidus/siccus, ὑγρός/ξηρός). Each substance has a position on both axes; each disease and constitution has a humoral-qualitative profile.

*The degrees*: Galen's _gradus_ system grades the intensity of a quality on a four-step scale (1st through 4th degree). Identical to Avicenna's Arabic _darajāt_.

=== Pharmacy preparations (Latin)

*Aqua* — water; either plain or as a distilled water (Aqua Foeniculi, Aqua Anisi, Aqua Rosarum).

*Cataplasma* — poultice / cataplasm; applied warm to the skin.

*Confectio* — confection; sweetened preparation; the Latin ancestor of the Arabic maʿjūn.

*Decoctum* — decoction.

*Electuarium* — electuary; sweet semi-solid medicated paste.

*Extractum* — extract (semi-solid or dried concentrate).

*Infusum* — infusion.

*Linctus* — cough-syrup (literally "lick"); the ancestor of modern cough syrups.

*Mel* — honey.

*Mel rosatum* — rose honey.

*Oleum* — oil (medicated or simple).

*Oxymel / Oxymeli* (Greek-Latin) — honey-and-vinegar preparation; the Hippocratic substance descended to Arabic _sakanjabīn_.

*Pilula* — pill.

*Pulvis* — powder.

*Sirupus / Syrupus* — syrup; descended from Arabic _sharāb_ / _sharbat_.

*Spiritus* — alcoholic distillate.

*Succus* — juice or extracted liquid. _Succus liquiritiae_ — licorice extract.

*Tinctura* — tincture (alcohol-based extract).

*Trochiscus (pl. trochisci)* — lozenge / pastille.

*Unguentum* — ointment.

*Mellis* (gen. of mel) and *mellites* — honey-based pharmacy preparations.

=== Greek-pharmacology vocabulary used throughout

*φαρμακόν (pharmakón)* — drug / remedy / poison (the polyvalent classical Greek pharmacological word).

*ἁπλᾶ φάρμακα (haplá phármaka, "simple drugs")* — the simple-drug category that Avicenna's Latin translators rendered as _simplicia_ and the Arabic _mufradāt_.

*φακή (phakḗ)* — lentil.

*κριθή (krithḗ)* — barley.

*πυρός (pyrós)* — wheat.

*οἶνος (oînos)* — wine.

*μέλι (méli)* — honey.

*ἔλαιον (élaion)* — olive oil.

*ζίγγιβερις (zíngiberis)* — ginger.

*γλυκύρριζα (glykýrrhiza)* — licorice ("sweet root").

*κύμινον (kýminon)* — cumin.

*κορίαννον (kóriannon)* — coriander.

*ἄνισον (ánison)* — anise.

*μάραθον (máraθon)* — fennel.

*σκόροδον (skórodon)* — garlic. The Hippocratic-Dioscoridean foundational term.

*βλήχων (blēchōn)* — pennyroyal (_Mentha pulegium_). Hippocratic-Dioscoridean term; the Galenic "warming and drying in the 3rd degree" classification; the source of the Arabic _fūdhanaj_ tradition. See _poejo.typ_.

*ἀψίνθιον / ἄψινθος (apsínthion / ápsinthos)* — *wormwood* (_Artemisia absinthium_); the proverbially bitter herb of antiquity, of uncertain (likely pre-Greek/loan) origin. _apsínthion_ is Dioscorides' and Galen's pharmacological term (→ Latin _absinthium_ → German _Wermut_ → English _vermouth_); _ápsinthos_ is the biblical form, the apocalyptic star "Wormwood" of Revelation 8:11 (Hebrew _laʿănāh_ in the OT bitterness-metaphor). See _wormwood.typ_.

*ἀψινθίτης οἶνος (apsinthítēs oînos) / vinum absinthites* — "wine of wormwood"; the Dioscoridean stomachic-and-appetite wine (_De Materia Medica_ III.23), the direct classical ancestor of *vermouth* (named from German _Wermut_, wormwood) and of the wormwood-flavored *amari*; continued in Tibb as *Sharāb al-afsantīn*. See _wormwood.typ_.

*Amarum (pl. amara)* — Latin pharmacy term for a *simple bitter tonic* taken before meals to stimulate appetite and digestion (the bitter-secretagogue class); wormwood is the archetypal _amarum_. Cognate register: the Italian *amaro* (see cross-tradition concordance), the Tibb _muqawwī al-maʿida_ bitters, and the dravyaguna _dīpana-pācana_ bitters.

*καρυόφυλλον (karyóphyllon, "nut-leaf")* — _káryon_ ("nut") + _phýllon_ ("leaf"); the ancient Greek-Latin name for the *clove* (→ Latin _caryophyllum_, old genus _Caryophyllus_, species epithet _caryophyllata_; Arabic _qaranful_; and, via Old French _gilofre_, English *gillyflower*, the clove-scented pink). Pliny's imported aromatic _caryophyllon_ (_Nat. Hist._ XII). See _cloves.typ_.

*Juglans (< Jōvis glāns, "Jupiter's nut")* — the Latin genus name for *walnut*, the "nut of Jove," ranked above the common acorn; hence _Juglans regia_ (the Old-World Persian walnut) and _J. nigra_ (American black walnut). Cf. Greek _káryon basilikón_ ("royal nut") in Dioscorides. See _black-walnut.typ_.

*σταφίς (staphís)* — *raisin* (dried grape); cf. *σταφυλή (staphylḗ)* grape-cluster, *βότρυς (bótrys)* cluster, *ἄμπελος (ámpelos)* vine. Dioscorides' term for the raisin, used for mouth, throat, cough, and kidneys; Latin _uva passa_ (< _passus_, "spread out to dry"). See _raisins.typ_.

*λύκιον (lýkion)* — a *false friend*: the classical Greek _lykion_ (Dioscorides I.100) is an astringent eye-medicine extracted from a thorny _Rhamnus_/_Berberis_ shrub of *Lycia*, *not* the Solanaceous genus _Lycium_ to which Linnaeus later transferred the name. The Chinese goji berry (_Lycium barbarum_) has *no* classical Greek pedigree; that both are "eye" drugs is coincidence. See _goji-berry.typ_.

*ἐλελίσφακον / σφάκος (elelísphakon / sphákos)* — *sage* (_Salvia officinalis_ and eastern-Mediterranean sages); Dioscorides' name (III.33), of uncertain pre-Greek origin; modern Greek *φασκόμηλο (faskómilo)* continues it. The Latin genus name _salvia_ is unrelated in form — from _salvus_/_salvēre_ ("to be well," "the healing herb"), cognate with _salvation_/_salve_/_safe_ — and encodes the panacea reputation of the Salernitan maxim (_"Cur moriatur homo cui salvia crescit in horto?"_). See _sage.typ_.

*μῆλον (mêlon) / mālum* — *apple* (_Malus_) and, generically, *tree-fruit / round fruit* in both Greek and Latin — the sense that generated quince (_mēlon kydōnion_), peach (_mālum persicum_, "Persian apple"), apricot (_mālum armeniacum_), citron (_mēlon mēdikon_), and pomegranate (_mālum granatum_), and also — via *χαμαίμηλον (chamaímēlon, "ground-apple")*, χαμαί "on the ground" + mêlon — *chamomile*, named for the apple-like scent of its crushed flowers (Dioscorides, Galen). The Latin near-homophony _mālum_ (apple) / _malum_ (evil) is the pun behind the Edenic-apple identification (the Genesis fruit is unnamed). See _apple.typ_, _chamomile.typ_.

*ἄπιον (ápion) / pirum* — *pear* (_Pyrus_); _achrás_ (ἀχράς) the wild pear. Both the Greek and Latin words are probably a *pre-Indo-European Mediterranean substrate borrowing*, shared rather than inherited. Classed cooling-and-astringent, "heavy raw, best cooked" (Galen, Pliny). See _pear.typ_.

*χαμαίμηλον (chamaímēlon, "ground-apple") / ἀνθεμίς (anthemís)* — *chamomile*; χαμαί ("low, on the ground") + μῆλον ("apple"), for the apple-like scent of the crushed flowers — the same μῆλον root as the apple-family names above. Dioscorides (_De Materia Medica_ III) describes several *anthemis* and *chamaímēlon* kinds by flower colour, and *their mapping onto the modern species (_Matricaria chamomilla_, _Chamaemelum nobile_, _Anthemis_ spp.) is not securely resolved* — the ancient terms covered a cluster of similar Asteraceae; flagged rather than tidied. Galen classes chamomile *warm and drying*, and *ἔλαιον χαμαιμήλινον* (Latin _oleum chamomillae_), the infused chamomile oil, is a standard Galenic resolvent-analgesic topical and the ancestor of the Unani *roghan-i bābūnah*. Modern Greek *χαμομήλι (chamomíli)*. See _chamomile.typ_.

*χρυσός + ἄνθεμον (chrysós + ánthemon, "gold-flower")* — the elements of the Linnaean genus *Chrysanthemum* (1753). *A flagged false friend:* Linnaeus founded the name on *Mediterranean* yellow daisies (the "crown daisy" group, now _Glebionis_), not on the East Asian cultivated chrysanthemum, to which later botanists extended it — so the Greek "gold" describes neither the Chinese drug nor its premium *white* medicinal grades, and the Greek name supplies the Chinese plant with no classical pedigree. Cf. _lýkion_/_Lycium_ and _kóïx_/_Coix_. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*κόϊξ (kóïx)* — in Theophrastus and other classical authors, an *Egyptian palm* (generally identified as a _Hyphaene_ doum palm). Linnaeus reused the word for the unrelated Asian grass *Coix lacryma-jobi* (Job's tears) — a loose recycling of an available classical name, carrying *no* implication of ancient attestation for the Asian plant. The third of this book's Linnaean false friends, with _Lycium_ and _Chrysanthemum_. See _coix.typ_.

*ἄρον (áron) / δρακόντιον (drakóntion)* — *Arum maculatum* (cuckoo-pint) and *Dracunculus*, the acrid Araceae of Dioscorides and Pliny: applied to swellings and sores, and taken internally *only after boiling or drying* to moderate the acridity. The same corrective principle as the Chinese 炮製 and the Ayurvedic *śodhana*, reached independently. The European tail of the story is *Portland sago*, a starch washed out of _Arum maculatum_ tubers in 18th–19th c. Dorset and sold as an invalid food — a cottage industry whose whole method is the separation of starch from the irritant fraction. See _ban-xia.typ_.

*ἰνδικόν (indikón) / indicum* — "the Indian [dye]": the Greek and Latin name for *indigo*, naming the substance by its *provenance* — whence Spanish/Portuguese *índigo* and English *indigo*. Dioscorides and Pliny know it as an imported *pigment*, with only marginal medicinal use; the genuinely medicinal Western plant of this family is *woad* (_Isatis tinctoria_, Latin *glastum*), for which see `ban-lan-gen.typ`. *Note the double etymology:* the same substance reached the West twice under two names — *indigo* by provenance (Greek), and *anil* by colour (Sanskrit *नील* → Arabic *نيل* → Iberian *añil/anil*), the latter giving the chemical term *aniline*. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*ἀμάραντος (amárantos, "unfading")* — amaranth; literally "the unfading flower"; the classical Greek-Roman symbol of *immortality* and the source of the Pauline NT _ἀμάραντος_ epithet (1 Peter 1:4, 5:4 — "inheritance unfading," "crown of unfading glory"). The classical Greco-Roman *ornamental* register; the seed-as-food use is American and post-Columbian to the Mediterranean. See _amaranth.typ_.

*Moretum (moretum)* — Latin garlic-and-herb-and-cheese-and-oil paste; the foundational rustic Mediterranean food, described in the *Moretum* poem attributed to Virgil's youth. The cultural-and-pharmaceutical foundation of the modern Mediterranean *aïoli / alioli / toum / skordalia* tradition. See _garlic.typ_.

*Pulegium / puleium* — Latin for pennyroyal (Pliny etymology from _pulex_, "flea" — the flea-driving attribution). See _poejo.typ_.

*Theriac Andromachi* — the imperial-Roman universal-antidote of Nero's physician Andromachus the Elder; ~60 ingredients including viper-flesh. The most-famous *theriac* (Arabic _tiryāq_, → "Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ"). Continued in medieval European pharmacy through to the 19th c.

*Mithridatium* — the legendary universal-antidote of *Mithridates VI of Pontus* (134-63 BCE), reportedly developed by sub-toxic-dosing all known poisons; the legendary "mithridatic" tradition of self-immunization against poison. The Roman *Theriac Andromachi* is the direct successor.

*Bald's Leechbook* — the foundational Anglo-Saxon (10th c.) medical-recipe collection; documented use of garlic in numerous preparations. The famous 2015 Nottingham reconstruction of Bald's eye-salve (garlic + onion + wine + bile) demonstrated genuine antibiotic activity against modern MRSA. See _garlic.typ_.

*Gārlēac (Old English)* — "spear-leek"; _gār_ (spear) + _lēac_ (leek/generic Allium); the Anglo-Saxon etymology of English "garlic." See _garlic.typ_.

*Matricaria (< mātrīx / māter, "womb, mother")* — the Linnaean genus name for *chamomile*, encoding the plant's traditional standing as a *uterine and gynaecological remedy* (emmenagogue) — one of the clearest cases in Western pharmacobotany of a botanical name preserving a classical indication, and one where the etymology and the modern pregnancy caution point the same way. See _chamomile.typ_.

*Goober / pindar (English, < Bantu)* — *peanut* in the American South: *goober* from Kikongo/Kimbundu *nguba*, *pindar* from Kikongo *mpinda*. Among the clearest surviving *Bantu loanwords in American English*, and evidence of the peanut's route: South America → West Africa (Portuguese shipping) → North America, the last leg with enslaved West and Central Africans who carried the crop and its names together. *The crop widely thought of as native to the American South arrived by way of Angola and the Congo.* See _peanut.typ_.

*Tuckahoe / "Indian bread" (English, < Algonquian)* — names applied in North America to edible underground masses: properly the arum root _Peltandra virginica_, extended by settlers to the sclerotia of *_Wolfiporia cocos_* — the *same fungus* as Chinese 茯苓, dug and eaten as a starchy famine food. *Independent convergent ethnomycology, not a transmitted tradition*: the use was alimentary, with none of the Chinese drug's part-splitting or formula pharmacology. See _fuling.typ_.

*China root (radix chinae)* — *a disambiguation, not a synonym*: in 16th–18th-c. European pharmacy "China root" denotes *_Smilax china_*, a sarsaparilla-class drug imported for syphilis, gout, and chronic skin disease. It is *not* Poria (茯苓), and modern English sources that call poria "China root" import a false pedigree. See _fuling.typ_.

*Pluche (eponym)* — Noël Antoine Pluche (1688-1761), French naturalist; eponym of the genus *_Pluchea_* (Asteraceae). See _lucera.typ_.

*πάναξ / πανάκεια (pánax / panákeia, "all-heal")* — πᾶν ("all") + ἄκος ("cure, remedy"); the source of English *panacea*, and in myth *Panákeia*, a daughter of Asclepius and goddess of universal remedy. Linnaeus took it as the genus name *Panax* for ginseng (1753, on the American species). *Note the contrast with this book's Greek-named false friends* (_Coix_, _Chrysanthemum_, _Lycium_): _Panax_ is not an ancient plant-name misapplied to an Asian species but a *deliberate descriptive coinage* asserting the drug's reputation — apt rather than accidental, though "panacea" remains a claim and not a finding. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*Mandragora (mandrake)* — the Mediterranean tropane-alkaloid Solanaceae whose *anthropomorphic forked root* made it the Old World's other great man-shaped drug, hedged with harvest taboo and ritual. Recorded here as a *parallel, not a connection*: ginseng (人參, "human root") and mandrake arrive independently at the same signature-reasoning, but there is no historical link, and mandrake is genuinely toxic where ginseng is not. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*Adaptogen* — a *20th-century Soviet pharmacological coinage* (Nikolai Lazarev, 1947; elaborated by Israil Brekhman from the 1950s) for substances held to raise nonspecific resistance to stress. *Panax ginseng* was the original and paradigm subject, with eleuthero, schisandra, and rhodiola following. *Not a classical category in any tradition in this book*, and not a regulatory one: useful as a research heuristic, misleading when it recasts a pattern-matched tonic as universally applicable. See _schisandra.typ_, _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*"Siberian ginseng"* — *a misnomer*: the plant is *eleuthero*, _Eleutherococcus senticosus_ (Araliaceae), not a _Panax_ and not a ginseng; the name was popularized through the Soviet adaptogen literature and the supplement trade. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*ἀσπάραγος (aspáragos)* — asparagus.

*τρίβολος (tríbolos)* — tribulus / caltrop.

*ψυχρός / θερμός / ὑγρός / ξηρός (psychrós / thermós / hygrós / xērós)* — cold / hot / moist / dry (the four primary qualities).

*εὔκρασις / δυσκρασία (eúkrasis / dyskrasía)* — good-mixture (= health) / bad-mixture (= disease) — the humoral-balance concept.

=== Classical source-texts

*Hippocratic Corpus* — the broad attribution covering ~60 works of the 5th–4th c. BCE Greek medical school. _On Regimen in Acute Diseases_, _On the Sacred Disease_, _Epidemics_, etc.

*Theophrastus, Historia Plantarum* — _Enquiry into Plants_, 4th c. BCE; the foundational botanical text.

*Dioscorides, De Materia Medica* — 1st c. CE; the foundational Greek pharmacology; ~600 medicinal substances; Beck translation (Olms-Weidmann, 2005) is the standard modern English.

*Pliny, Naturalis Historia* — 1st c. CE; the encyclopaedic Latin natural history with extensive pharmacology.

*Galen* — 2nd c. CE; numerous works including _De alimentorum facultatibus_ (_On the Properties of Foodstuffs_), _De simplicium medicamentorum facultatibus_, _De temperamentis_. The foundational Greco-Roman pharmacology.

*Hildegard von Bingen, Physica* — 12th c.; medieval European medical-botanical treatise.

*Avicenna's Canon of Medicine* (Latin tradition: _Liber Canonis_, Gerard of Cremona's 12th-c. translation) — the principal medieval European reception of Arabic Tibb.

== Japanese Tea-and-Medicine Tradition

*喫茶養生記 (Kissa Yōjōki, "Notes on Drinking Tea for Nourishing Life")* — Eisai's 1211 work; the foundational Japanese tea-as-medicine text. Presented to the shogun Sanetomo.

*抹茶 (matcha)* — powdered shade-grown green tea; the foundational tea-ceremony tea.

*煎茶 (sencha)* — standard Japanese green tea; sun-grown leaves; steamed-rolled-dried.

*玉露 (gyokuro)* — premium shade-grown Japanese green tea; ~3 weeks of shading before harvest; sweet, umami, low-tannin.

*番茶 (bancha)* — late-harvest mature-leaf Japanese green tea; the everyday household tea.

*焙じ茶 (hōjicha)* — roasted bancha; brown rather than green; low-caffeine, comforting.

*玄米茶 (genmaicha)* — bancha or sencha + roasted brown rice.

*京番茶 (kyōbancha)* — Kyoto-region bancha; pan-toasted with distinctive smoky character.

*茎茶 (kukicha)* / *棒茶 (bocha)* — stalk tea; made from stems and twigs; very low caffeine.

*三年番茶 (sannen bancha, "three-year bancha")* — aged bancha; the canonical macrobiotic daily tea.

*梅醤番茶 (ume-shō bancha)* — bancha + umeboshi salt-plum + tamari + grated ginger; macrobiotic folk medicine.

*鮭 (sake / shake)* — salmon (Japanese).

*鰯 (iwashi)* — sardine / small clupeoid fish.

*煮干し (niboshi)* — dried baby sardines used for dashi.

*鰹節 (katsuobushi)* — dried fermented bonito for dashi.

*殺青 (shāqīng / Japanese: satsusei)* — the kill-green tea-processing step (denaturation of polyphenol oxidase). Pan-fried in Chinese tradition; steamed in Japanese (蒸す _mushi_).

*赤飯 (sekihan, "red rice")* — short-grain glutinous rice steamed with adzuki beans; auspicious-occasion food.

*餡子 (anko)* — sweetened adzuki-bean paste; the foundational Japanese confectionery filling.

*お汁粉 (oshiruko)* / *ぜんざい (zenzai)* — sweet adzuki soup with rice cakes.

*酥油茶 (Japanese: suyu cha; Tibetan-Mandarin: sūyóu chá)* — Tibetan butter tea; the brick-tea + yak-butter + salt staple drink.

*ふぐ (fugu)* — puffer fish; the deliberately-controlled tetrodotoxin tradition.

*鳩麦 / ハトムギ (hatomugi, "pigeon wheat")* — Job's tears (_Coix lacryma-jobi_) seed; the standard Japanese name and the roasted grain beverage *hatomugi-cha*. Also a major ingredient in Japanese and Korean skincare, on *weak* ("skin-whitening") evidence — flagged as a commercial register. See _coix.typ_.

*ヨクイニン (yokuinin)* — the Sino-Japanese reading of 薏苡仁 (Job's tears), and in Japan the name of a *specific widely used indication*: oral yokuinin preparations are sold over the counter and prescribed for *warts (verruca vulgaris)*, with a modest clinical literature and a proposed immunomodulatory mechanism — a modern Japanese development out of the classical 解毒散結 register. See _coix.typ_.

*茯苓 (ブクリョウ, bukuryō)* — Poria in Kampo, where it is among the most-used drugs (_Goreisan_ 五苓散, _Rikkunshito_ 六君子湯, _Keishibukuryogan_ 桂枝茯苓丸, _Hochuekkito_). See _fuling.typ_.

*四君子湯 / 六君子湯 (Shikunshitō / Rikkunshitō)* — the Kampo readings of Four Gentlemen Decoction and its phlegm-damp expansion. *Rikkunshitō* is among the most prescribed Kampo formulas in Japan and carries *the best formula-level clinical evidence* of the family — randomized trials in functional dyspepsia and chemotherapy-induced nausea/anorexia, with a proposed *ghrelin-signalling* mechanism. That the evidence is Japanese rather than Chinese owes much to Kampo's standardization of composition and dose. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*菊 (kiku)* — chrysanthemum in Japan: the *imperial emblem* (*菊花紋章, kikka monshō*, the sixteen-petal crest of the Imperial House), the Double-Ninth *菊の節句 (kiku no sekku)* festival, the edible petals *食用菊 (shokuyōgiku)* of autumn cuisine, and the tea *菊花茶 (kikkacha)*. The pharmacological use follows the Chinese; the symbolic and culinary registers are distinctively Japanese. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*茼蒿 / 春菊 (shungiku)* — "chrysanthemum greens," the *leaf vegetable* _Glebionis coronaria_ — a *different species* from the medicinal chrysanthemum flower, and a frequent point of confusion. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*朝鮮人参 / 高麗人参 (chōsen ninjin / kōrai ninjin)* — ginseng in Japanese, and *the qualifier is obligatory*: in modern Japanese *人参 (ninjin)* alone means *carrot*, the word having been transferred to the European root vegetable on a shape resemblance. A Japanese recipe calling for 人参 wants carrots. Red ginseng is *紅参 (kōjin)*. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*半夏生 (hangeshō)* — one of the *雑節 (zassetsu)*, the supplementary seasonal markers of the traditional Japanese agricultural calendar: roughly 1–2 July, the eleventh day after the summer solstice, *named for the emergence of 半夏 (pinellia)*. It carried a real deadline — *rice transplanting had to be finished by hangeshō* — and regional customs attach to it, including the Kansai practice of eating octopus that day. A medicinal weed giving its name to a calendar date and a farming deadline, descending from the same phenological observation as the Chinese name 半夏 ("half summer"). See _ban-xia.typ_.

*練り胡麻 / 胡麻豆腐 (neri goma / goma-dōfu)* — *neri goma*, "kneaded sesame," the Japanese sesame paste, made white (白練り胡麻) or black (黒練り胡麻), roasted and ground very smooth; the base of *胡麻だれ (goma-dare)* dressings and *胡麻和え (goma-ae)*. *胡麻豆腐 (goma-dōfu)* — sesame "tofu," neri goma set with kudzu starch: a staple of Buddhist temple cuisine (*精進料理, shōjin ryōri*) and one of the few dishes in which sesame paste is the food rather than a condiment. See _sesame-seeds.typ_.

*藍 / 藍染 / すくも (ai / aizome / sukumo)* — *ai* the indigo plant (_Polygonum tinctorium_, 蓼藍) and the colour; *aizome* indigo dyeing; *sukumo* the composted leaf-mass of the *阿波藍 (awa-ai)* tradition of Tokushima, fermented over months before vatting. The drug *青黛 (seitai)* and the dye come from the same plant and region but by *different processes for different ends* — conflating them overstates the medical tradition. The phrase *"Japan blue"* belongs to the dye register. See _qing-dai.typ_.

*イズミダイ (izumidai, "spring sea-bream")* — a Japanese *market euphemism* for tilapia, sold as a sea-bream substitute; recorded because it illustrates tilapia's status as a generic white-fish commodity rather than a culturally situated food. See _tilapia.typ_.

== Korean

_Korean terms used in the entries. The Korean pharmacological tradition (*한의학, hanuihak*, "Eastern medicine") shares the Chinese canon but has its own classical literature and its own materia medica emphases — most of all for ginseng, where Korea is the primary rather than a derivative tradition._

=== Classical source-texts

*동의보감 (Donguibogam, 東醫寶鑑, "Precious Mirror of Eastern Medicine")* — compiled by *허준 (Heo Jun)* and published in *1613*; the foundational Korean medical compendium, synthesizing Chinese sources with Korean practice and native drugs, and inscribed on the UNESCO Memory of the World register. The text in which the Korean handling of 인삼 is codified. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*향약집성방 (Hyangyak jipseongbang, 鄉藥集成方, "Compilation of Native Korean Prescriptions")* — 1433, early Joseon; the compendium that established Korean materia medica as a tradition attending to *its own flora* (_hyangyak_, "native drugs") rather than importing Chinese drug-lists wholesale. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

=== Ginseng vocabulary

*인삼 (insam, 人蔘)* — ginseng (_Panax ginseng_); nationally *고려인삼 (Goryeo insam)*, after the *Goryeo* dynasty (918–1392) — the same dynastic name from which "Korea" derives, so that plant and country share an etymology. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*수삼 / 백삼 / 홍삼 / 흑삼 / 태극삼 (susam / baeksam / hongsam / heuksam / taegeuksam)* — the Korean *processing-derived drug-forms*: *수삼* (水蔘) fresh "water ginseng"; *백삼* (白蔘) peeled and sun-dried "white"; *홍삼* (紅蔘) *steamed then dried* "red" — the signature Korean product, read as warmer and more strongly tonifying, and the form whose ginsenoside transformation modern chemistry has documented; *흑삼* (黑蔘) repeatedly steamed "black" (the 九蒸九晒 logic, cf. rehmannia); *태극삼* (太極蔘) briefly scalded, intermediate. A saṃskāra system on a par with 生薑/乾薑 ginger. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _ginger.typ_, _rehmannia.typ_.

*산삼 / 장뇌삼 (sansam / jangnoesam)* — *wild* mountain ginseng (山蔘), a class apart in rarity and price, and *semi-wild* ginseng sown and left in mountain forest. *심마니 (simmani)* — the wild-ginseng gatherers, who observe ritual preparation and taboo and call out *"심봤다!" (simbwatda,* "I've found ginseng!") on a discovery. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*경옥고 (Gyeongokgo, 瓊玉膏, "Jade Spring Paste")* — the great Korean tonic *膏* (paste/electuary): ginseng with fresh rehmannia juice, white poria, and honey, long-decocted to a dense paste and taken by the spoonful as a longevity preparation. Recorded in the _Donguibogam_. See _korean-ginseng.typ_, _rehmannia.typ_, _fuling.typ_, _honey.typ_.

*삼계탕 (samgyetang, 蔘鷄湯)* — ginseng-chicken soup: a young chicken stuffed with ginseng, glutinous rice, jujube, garlic and usually *황기* (astragalus), simmered whole. Eaten on *복날 (boknal)*, the three hottest days of summer, on the 暑傷氣 / *이열치열* reasoning — see those entries and _korean-ginseng.typ_, _astragalus.typ_.

*이열치열 (以熱治熱, iyeolchiyeol, "treat heat with heat")* — the Korean name of the principle behind the 복날 custom; see the Chinese-medicine section entry 以熱治熱.

*홍삼정 (hongsamjeong)* — red-ginseng concentrate, the dominant modern commercial form; *인삼차 (insam-cha)* ginseng tea; *인삼주 (insam-ju)* ginseng liquor; *정과 (jeonggwa)* honey-candied root. *정관장 (Jung Kwan Jang)* — the principal modern brand, descending institutionally from a state ginseng monopoly. Production centres: historically *개성 (Gaeseong)*, today *금산 (Geumsan)*, *강화 (Ganghwa)*, *풍기 (Punggi)*.

=== Other

*황기 (hwanggi)* — astragalus (黃芪); see _astragalus.typ_.

*사군자탕 (sagunjatang, 四君子湯)* — the Korean reading of Four Gentlemen Decoction; a standard prescription in 한의학 as elsewhere in the Sinitic medical world. See _four-gentlemen-decoction.typ_.

*율무 (yulmu)* — Job's tears (_Coix lacryma-jobi_); *율무차 (yulmu-cha)*, a thick sweetened Job's-tears drink, is a standard Korean traditional beverage, usually taken hot — the roasted/thickened form being the climatically apt adaptation of a cooling grain to a cold-winter zone. See _coix.typ_.

*국화 (gukhwa)* — chrysanthemum; *국화차 (gukhwacha)*, chrysanthemum tea, a standard Korean traditional tea. See _chrysanthemum.typ_.

*복령 (bongnyeong)* — Poria (茯苓) in Korean medicine. See _fuling.typ_.

== Brazilian / Portuguese / Indigenous Amazonian

*Rapadura* — Brazilian / Lusophone artisanal unrefined whole-cane sugar. From Portuguese _rapar_, "to scrape." Closely related to Mexican _piloncillo_, Colombian _panela_, Indian _guḍa_, Chinese _hóngtáng_.

*Açúcar mascavo* — partially-refined intermediate whole-cane sugar.

*Cafezinho* — small-cup coffee; Brazilian household tradition.

*Garrafada* — Brazilian folk-medical decoction; a generic compound preparation in a glass bottle.

*Erva-doce* — ambiguous Brazilian Portuguese term — in Northeast Brazil typically anise (_Pimpinella anisum_); in Southeast and South Brazil typically fennel (_Foeniculum vulgare_). The disambiguation matters.

*Funcho* — unambiguous Brazilian Portuguese for fennel.

*Sertão* / *sertanejo* — the semi-arid backlands of Northeast Brazil; the cultural region of rapadura, cangaceiros, cordel literature.

*Sampaguita* — _Jasminum sambac_; Philippine national flower; from Spanish-Tagalog naming.

*Melati* — Indonesian for _Jasminum sambac_; Indonesian national flower.

*Moqueca* — Brazilian (especially Bahian) fish-and-palm-oil-and-coconut stew.

*Bacalhau* — Portuguese / Brazilian salt-cured Atlantic cod; foundational to Portuguese-Brazilian cuisine.

*Pirarucu* — _Arapaima gigas_; iconic Amazonian freshwater fish.

*Amendoim* — *peanut* in Portuguese, from Tupi *mandubi / manobi* — an indigenous South American name for a South American crop, surviving in the colonial language. Brazilian preparations: *paçoca* (crushed peanut, cassava flour and sugar pressed into a crumbly block) and *pé-de-moleque* (peanut brittle), both canonical *Festa Junina* confections; peanuts also enter Bahian *vatapá* and *caruru*. See _peanut.typ_.

*Tilápia / tilápia-do-nilo* — tilapia (_Oreochromis niloticus_); now among the largest Brazilian aquaculture products, having *overtaken native species in farmed-fish volume*, and a staple of inland Brazilian cooking (_tilápia na brasa_, _frita_, in _moqueca_). *Also an invasive species* in Brazilian natural waters, with documented displacement of native fish — a conservation concern that stands alongside, and is not cancelled by, the fish's nutritional and economic value. See _tilapia.typ_.

*Tucupi* — fermented manioc juice; Amazonian Tupi cooking foundation.

*Vinho de açaí* — açaí pulp / juice; Northern-Brazilian everyday food.

*ɨwasaʔi* (Tupi-Guarani) — "the fruit that weeps water"; etymological source of Brazilian Portuguese _açaí_.

*wara'ná* (Saterê-Mawé) — etymological source of Brazilian Portuguese _guaraná_.

*pasta de guaraná* / *bastão de guaraná* (Brazilian Portuguese) / *içapó* (Saterê-Mawé) — the traditional roasted-pounded guaraná seed paste, formed into hard sticks for storage.

*Pão-de-queijo* — Brazilian cheese-bread (not in this materia medica directly, but the Brazilian-cuisine cultural register).

*Vatapá* / *acarajé* / *moqueca capixaba* — Brazilian regional dishes (not in entries but cultural register).

*Caboclo* — Amazonian / Brazilian person of mixed indigenous-European descent; the rural Amazonian / Northeastern cultural population.

*Cangaceiro* — Northeast-Brazilian bandit-itinerant of the early 20th c.; rapadura was the foundational cangaceiro energy food.

*Cordel* — Northeast-Brazilian popular oral / printed literature tradition.

*Coalhada* — Brazilian curd / yogurt.

*Poejo* — Portuguese-Brazilian for *pennyroyal* (_Mentha pulegium_); from Latin _puleium_. Foundational Portuguese-Brazilian folk-medical aromatic; canonical in *chá de poejo* (pennyroyal tea), *açorda à alentejana* (Alentejan bread-soup with garlic, olive oil, water, poejo, and poached egg), and the *resguardo* postpartum register. *Poejo-rasteiro* — prostrate form; *Poejo-do-campo* — regional Brazilian alternative species (_Cunila microcephala_). See _poejo.typ_ for the species-identification disambiguation.

*Camomila (Portuguese)* — chamomile (_Matricaria chamomilla_); one of the most universally known Brazilian-Portuguese household herbs, central to _chá de camomila_ for infant colic, calming, and sleep. See _chamomile.typ_.

*Resguardo (Portuguese)* — postpartum rest period (~30 days); the canonical Iberian-Lusophone postpartum-confinement tradition during which poejo tea, light foods, and rest constitute the recovery regimen.

*Açorda* — Portuguese (especially Alentejan) bread-and-water-and-aromatic soup; the foundational rural Portuguese dish. *Açorda à alentejana* is the canonical *poejo*-and-garlic-and-olive-oil-and-egg version.

*Quitoco* — Argentine-Uruguayan-Paraguayan-Southern-Brazilian Hispano-Lusophone for *Pluchea sagittalis*; foundational Río-de-la-Plata digestive bitter. From Guaraní *kytoko*. See _lucera.typ_.

*Lucera / yerba lucera (Portuguese-Spanish)* — alternative names for *Pluchea sagittalis* / quitoco; the user-supplied identifier of the entry. Brazilian regional usage.

*Paratudo (Portuguese, "for-everything")* — generic Brazilian folk-medicine term applied to several species; sometimes _Pluchea sagittalis_, sometimes other panaceas. Species ambiguity is real.

*Madre-cravo, santa-maria, erva-de-santa-maria* — regional Brazilian-Portuguese names with substantial *species ambiguity* — these names are applied to _Pluchea sagittalis_, _Dysphania ambrosioides_ (Mexican-tea / wormseed), and other unrelated species in different regions. Specify by binomial when using these names.

*Chimarrão* — the canonical Brazilian-southern (Rio Grande do Sul) and Uruguayan / Argentine / Paraguayan *yerba-mate preparation* (_Ilex paraguariensis_) in a *cuia* (gourd) drunk through a *bomba* (metal straw). The foundational Río-de-la-Plata social-and-dietary substance; quitoco / lucera additions to chimarrão are a folk-medical extension.

*Asado (Argentine-Uruguayan)* / *Churrasco (Brazilian)* — the canonical Southern-Cone barbecue tradition; cultural-and-dietary context for the quitoco / lucera / chimarrão register.

*Resaca (Spanish) / Ressaca (Portuguese)* — hangover; the canonical indication for quitoco / lucera in Argentine-Uruguayan-Brazilian folk medicine.

*Yerbas digestivas (Spanish)* — "digestive herbs"; the broader Argentine-Uruguayan folk-medical category that includes quitoco / lucera, boldo (_Peumus boldus_), cedrón (_Aloysia citrodora_), manzanilla (_Matricaria chamomilla_), and others.

*Caña / Aguardiente / Grappa* — Latin-American cane-spirit / grape-spirit / regional distillates used as the maceration medium for quitoco / lucera and other South-American medicinal-bitter herbs in the *digestivo* tradition.

=== Mesoamerican (Aztec / Nahuatl) — Indigenous Amazonian / Latin American

*Huautli (Nahuatl)* — *amaranth seed*; foundational Aztec grain; the substance whose religious-ritual use led to the Spanish colonial suppression. See _amaranth.typ_.

*Tzoalli (Nahuatl)* — amaranth-flour-and-blood (or amaranth-flour-and-maguey-honey) paste; the foundational Aztec ritual substance for forming god-images during major festivals; suppressed post-Conquest. The modern *alegría* sweet is the form-survival.

*Alegría (Spanish, "joy / happiness")* — the modern Mexican popped-amaranth-and-honey / sugar / piloncillo sweet bar; survives the Aztec _tzoalli_ form with blood replaced by sweetener; widely associated with *Día de los Muertos* (1-2 November) in Mexican-Catholic folk practice.

*Día de los Muertos (Spanish)* — the Mexican Day of the Dead (1-2 November); the canonical Mexican folk-Catholic festival incorporating pre-Columbian and Catholic elements; *alegría* is among the foundational festival sweets.

*Cihuacōātl, Huitzilopochtli (Nahuatl)* — Aztec deities; Cihuacōātl the earth-mother-goddess, Huitzilopochtli the war-god; the principal deities whose images were formed from huautli-tzoalli paste during major festivals.

*Panquetzaliztli, Tóxcatl (Nahuatl)* — Aztec festival-month names; the principal festivals incorporating amaranth-image rituals.

*Tlālcacahuatl (Nahuatl, "earth cacao")* — *peanut*; *tlālli* "earth" + *cacahuatl* "cacao bean," naming the plant by its underground pod and its resemblance to the cacao bean. Source of Mexican Spanish *cacahuate* and French *cacahuète*. The Caribbean Taíno *maní* gave the usual word across most of Spanish-speaking South America, so Spanish inherited *two* indigenous names for one crop from two regions. See _peanut.typ_.

*Kiwicha (Quechua)* — *Andean amaranth*; the canonical Inca grain; foundational to the Andean (Peruvian, Bolivian, Ecuadorian) dietary tradition. The Quechua-language register parallels Nahuatl _huautli_ across the Mesoamerican-Andean divide.

*Pluchea (eponym genus)* — see Greco-Roman section.

*Kytoko / Ka'a guazu (Guaraní)* — Guaraní-language for *Pluchea sagittalis*; the indigenous-Guaraní source of the Hispano-Lusophone *quitoco* / *lucera*. See _lucera.typ_.

*Vrata (Sanskrit, in the Indian context)* — Hindu *fasting day*; the religious-dietary register that classifies pseudocereals (amaranth, buckwheat, water-chestnut flour) as *not-true-grain* and therefore *fasting-permitted*. See _amaranth.typ_, _buckwheat.typ_.

*Sabudana (Hindi)* / *Siṅghāḍā (Hindi)* — *sago* (tapioca pearl) and *water-chestnut flour*; canonical vrata foods accompanying amaranth (rāmdānā) and buckwheat (kuṭṭū) in Hindu fasting-day cuisine.

=== Afro-Brazilian (Bantu-derived) — Religious-and-medical heritage

*Mulungu (Portuguese, from Bantu Kimbundu / Kikongo "mulúngu")* — Brazilian native Fabaceae tree (_Erythrina mulungu_, _E. velutina_); the canonical Brazilian folk anxiolytic-and-sedative bark. The name is the *exceptional case of a Bantu (African) loanword reaching the international Linnaean botanical nomenclature* — preserved in the species epithet *_Erythrina mulungu_*. The substance's African-Brazilian cultural-and-medical transmission via the Atlantic slave trade preserved the term and the recognition of functionally-analogous African Erythrina species. See _mulungu.typ_.

*Mulúngu (Kimbundu) / Mulungu (Kikongo)* — Bantu polysemic term meaning variously *"tree / sky / god / sacred tree"* in the source West-Central African languages. Brought to Brazil through the 16th-19th-c. Atlantic slave trade and applied to the native Brazilian _Erythrina_. See _mulungu.typ_.

*Suinã (Tupi-derived Brazilian Portuguese)* — Northeast Brazilian for _Erythrina velutina_, the canonical Caatinga mulungu species. See _mulungu.typ_.

*Banho (Portuguese, "bath")* — ritual bath preparation in Afro-Brazilian Candomblé and Umbanda religious traditions; herbs (mulungu, assa-peixe, and others) are decocted and added to bath water for spiritual cleansing and protection. The cross-cultural recognition that pharmacologically-potent substances carry both medicinal and spiritual significance.

*Orixá (Yoruba-Bantu-derived Portuguese)* — *deity* in Afro-Brazilian religion (Candomblé, Umbanda); preserved from West African Yoruba *òrìṣà* with Bantu syncretism. Specific orixás (notably *Ossain*, the orixá of medicinal plants and herbal knowledge) are associated with specific substances. See _mulungu.typ_.

*Nkisi (Bantu Kongo)* — Kongolese spiritual-medicinal substance / spirit-vessel; the Bantu-religious term for sacred-and-medicinal substances; influenced the Afro-Brazilian religious-medical register. See _mulungu.typ_.

*Candomblé / Umbanda (Portuguese, from Bantu and African-syncretic sources)* — Afro-Brazilian religious traditions; foundational to the Brazilian religious-medical heritage of mulungu and other native substances. The Candomblé is the older / more-African; Umbanda is the syncretic Brazilian form combining Yoruba-Bantu orixás with Catholic saints and indigenous spirits.

=== Brazilian respiratory medicinals — substance-and-formula terms

*Assa-peixe (Portuguese, "fish-roaster")* — Brazilian Asteraceae respiratory herb (_Vernonanthura polyanthes_); the vernacular preserves the indigenous-Tupi-and-caboclo cooking-and-medicine tradition of using the inflorescence-stalks as fish-roasting skewers. See _assa-peixe.typ_.

*Guaco (Portuguese, from Cumanagoto / Carib-language family)* — Brazilian and South-American Asteraceae climbing vine (_Mikania glomerata_, _M. laevigata_); the canonical Brazilian respiratory and antitussive herb. The indigenous etymology preserves the original *snake-bite-curing* register; the modern Brazilian-pharmaceutical *xarope de guaco* is among the most-consumed over-the-counter respiratory products in Brazil. See _guaco.typ_.

*Cambará (Tupi-derived Brazilian Portuguese)* — *species-ambiguous* vernacular applied variously to _Vernonanthura polyanthes_ (assa-peixe), _Lantana camara_ (Verbenaceae, "cambará vermelho"), _Gochnatia polymorpha_ (Asteraceae, "cambará-do-mato"), and other unrelated species. Specify the binomial.

*Xarope de guaco (Portuguese, "guaco syrup")* — the canonical Brazilian commercial-pharmaceutical respiratory syrup; Anvisa-registered with standardized coumarin content; available in nearly every Brazilian pharmacy and supermarket. See _guaco.typ_.

*Mel de assa-peixe (Portuguese, "assa-peixe honey")* — the assa-peixe-blooming-season honey of Brazilian Southeast apiculture; collector-grade Brazilian artisanal product with respiratory-folk-medical valuation. See _assa-peixe.typ_.

*Mentruz (Brazilian Portuguese)* — _Lepidium virginicum_; canonical Brazilian folk respiratory companion herb. (Note: in some regions *mentruz* also refers to _Dysphania ambrosioides_ / Mexican-tea / wormseed — species ambiguity.)

*Eucalipto (Portuguese)* — eucalyptus (_Eucalyptus globulus_ and related); the canonical Brazilian respiratory companion herb to guaco.

*Maracujá (Portuguese, from Tupi)* — passion fruit (_Passiflora edulis_, _P. incarnata_); the canonical Brazilian folk anxiolytic companion to mulungu.

*Erva-cidreira (Portuguese)* — lemongrass / lemon balm; canonical Brazilian folk sedative companion herb.

*Cipó (Portuguese, from Tupi)* — *climbing vine*; general Brazilian botanical term for Asteraceae, Bignoniaceae, and other climbing-vine Brazilian native medicinals.

*Cumanagoto* — Carib-language indigenous Venezuelan people; etymological source of the name *guaco* (snake-bite-curing plant). The Humboldt-Bonpland 1799-1804 South-American expedition recorded the indigenous Venezuelan guaco use.

*Caatinga (Portuguese, from Tupi)* — the semi-arid scrub forest of Northeast Brazil; the canonical region for _Erythrina velutina_ (suinã), _Vernonia ferruginea_, and other characteristic Brazilian Northeast native medicinals.

*Cerrado (Portuguese)* — the Brazilian tropical-savanna ecoregion of central Brazil; the canonical region for many Brazilian native medicinals including _Vernonanthura polyanthes_ (assa-peixe-do-cerrado) and many Asteraceae species.

== Persian (cross-referenced to Tibb)

The Persian vocabulary largely overlaps with the Arabic Tibb vocabulary (the major medieval Arabic medical works are also Persian-medical authority). Distinctive Persian terms used in the materia medica:

*طب سنتی (ṭibb-e sonnati)* — Persian classical medicine (revived Iranian traditional medicine).

*شربت (sharbat)* — syrup; the Persian-origin word that gave Arabic *sharbat* and English *sherbet*.

*یاسمن (yāsamīn)* — jasmine; the etymological source of the Western jasmine word.

*انگدان / انگوژد (angudān / angū-zad)* — Persian for the asafoetida plant and the gum-resin.

*رازیانه (rāziyāne)* — fennel.

*زیره (zīrā / zīrah)* — cumin; etymological relative of Sanskrit jīraka.

*شیر (shīr)* — milk.

*برنج (berenj)* — rice.

*ماش (māsh)* — mung bean.

*یوسف افندی (yūsuf efendī)* — tangerine / mandarin in Egyptian Arabic, after Yusuf Pasha who introduced the cultivar in the 19th c.

*استکان (istikān)* — small Iranian tea-glass; the canonical Iranian tea-service vessel.

*سماور (samāvar)* — samovar; the Iranian / Russian tea-brewing water-and-tea apparatus.

*نبات (nabāt)* — Persian rock sugar.

== Cross-tradition concordance

Some convergent concepts mapped across the four major traditions:

*Cooling-and-cleansing*: 清熱 / pittahara, dāha-praśamana, śīta-vīrya / mubarriḍ, ʿalāj amrāḍ al-ṣafrā' / refrigerant, antipyretic, antiphlogistic.

*Warming-and-tonifying*: 補陽 / uṣṇa-vīrya rasāyana / mufariḥ, muqawwī al-jasad / hot-and-moist tonics.

*Deobstruent / channel-opening*: 通利 / srotośodhana / mufattiḥ.

*Carminative / digestive-aromatic*: 理氣 / dīpana-pācana, vāta-anulomana / kāsir li-l-riyāḥ, hāḍim / carminativus.

*Cardiotonic / mood-elevating*: 養心安神 / hṛdya, manas-prasādana / muqawwī al-qalb, mufariḥ, muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī / cordial.

*Aphrodisiac / virilifying*: 補腎助陽 / vājīkara, vṛṣya, śukrala / muqawwī al-bāh, muḥarrik al-bāh.

*Hepatic deobstruent*: 疏肝 / yakṛt-uttejaka / mufattiḥ al-kabid.

*Laxative (gentle)*: 潤腸通便 / mṛdu-bhedana / mulayyin.

*Diuretic*: 利水利尿 / mūtrala / mudirr li-l-bawl.

*Galactagogue*: 通乳 / stanya-janana / mudirr li-l-laban.

*Anthelminthic*: 殺蟲 / kṛmi-ghna / qātil li-l-dūd.

*Anti-toxin / antidote*: 解毒 / viṣa-ghna / muqāwim li-l-sumūm.

*Wound-healing*: 斂瘡 / vraṇa-ropaṇa / jurūḥ wa-quruḥ (the disease-category) with cleansing-and-healing actions.

*Vehicle / carrier (formulation principle)*: 調和諸藥 / anupāna, yogavāhī / muṣliḥ-and-vehicle principle.

*Constitution-by-temperament*: prakṛti / mizāj-mu'tadil and the eight imbalanced mizāj / krasis (Greek-Galenic) — the cross-system framework for individual-constitutional-pattern-based pharmacology.

*Process-creates-substance (saṃskāra)*: 炮製 (生/熟/炙/炮) / saṃskāra / muṣliḥ-by-design / Galenic _praeparationes_.

*Wrong-place-wrong-time-wrong-combination (compatibility)*: 配伍禁忌 / viruddha (deśa/kāla/saṃyoga/saṃskāra) / mawāne' (mizāj-mismatch).

*Universal antidote / theriac*: tiryāq (Arabic) / theriac (English-Latin) / Theriac Andromachi (Roman) / Mithridatium (Pontic Greek) — the cross-cultural concept of a complex compound polypharmaceutical against poison, plague, and humoral disorder; Avicenna gives a substantive Tibb treatment; the Mediterranean-Arabic-European pharmacy preserved the tradition into the 19th c. *Garlic as "Triyāq al-Fuqarāʾ"* — the poor-man's-theriac — is the cross-system class-substitution of a simple-substance for the elaborate compound. See _garlic.typ_.

*Apotropaic substance (against demons, evil spirits, the evil eye)*: bhūtaghna (Sanskrit) / bābūnaj-and-rue (Tibb folk) / garlic-against-vampires (European folk) — the cross-cultural recognition that pharmacologically-potent aromatic-pungent substances carry both medicinal and apotropaic-spiritual significance. Garlic is the most-attested cross-cultural example. See _garlic.typ_.

*Life-restoring in collapse*: 大補元氣, 回陽救逆 (獨參湯, 參附湯) / jīvanīya, ojas-vardhana, prāṇa-restoring / muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī — the category of drugs given when vitality itself is failing, as distinct from ordinary tonification. Ginseng is the cross-system exemplar. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*Anthropomorphic-root signature*: 人參 ("human root," the forked root graded by man-likeness) / European *mandrake* (_Mandragora_) — two unrelated traditions investing a man-shaped root with maximal potency and surrounding it with harvest taboo. *Parallel, not connection*; and mandrake is toxic where ginseng is not. See _korean-ginseng.typ_.

*Damp-draining / fluid-discharging*: 利水滲濕 (and the 淡味滲泄 principle) / mūtrala, śothahara / mudirr li-l-bawl, mujaffif / diuretic-and-desiccant. *Note the asymmetry:* the Chinese system makes *blandness itself* a drainage category; the six rasas have no equivalent (only _avyakta/alpa rasa_, the mere absence of taste). See _fuling.typ_, _coix.typ_.

*"Provoking" foods that re-surface latent disease*: 發物 (fāwù) / the classical contraindication of fish and heavy flesh in _kuṣṭha_ and Pitta-inflammatory states / muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ (humour-stirring). Three traditions independently isolate the same clinical caution — that certain rich or "stirring" foods bring latent skin, suppurative, and inflammatory disease to the surface. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*Convalescent broth*: 魚湯 / 鯽魚湯 (fish soup) / māṃsa-rasa / māʾ al-samak — a clean three-way convergence on *broth as the food of recovery* in Vāta-type or humoral depletion. See _fish.typ_, _tilapia.typ_.

*Pus-expelling / resolving internal suppuration*: 清熱排膿 / vraṇa-śodhana, śothahara / mukhrij al-qayḥ, muḥallil. See _coix.typ_.

*Digestive bitter (post-prandial)*: 健脾消食 / dīpana-pācana / muqawwī al-miʿda / Mediterranean amaro / South-American quitoco-and-aguardiente digestivo — the cross-cultural recognition of bitter-aromatic herbs as constitutionally appropriate post-heavy-meal aids. See _lucera.typ_.

